/* Sample essays (Grade One exemplars) for Modules 1–3 and Paper 3. Developed by Kester Small. */
window.COURSE_SAMPLES = {"m1": [{"prompt": "<p>Read the extract, then write an essay, in NO MORE than <b>500 words</b>, in which you</p><ul><li>state the writer's MAIN message in no more than 30 words</li><li>state the writer's purpose</li><li>discuss TWO organizational strategies OR TWO language techniques OR any combination of both which the writer uses to achieve the purpose</li><li>evaluate the credibility of the information presented.</li></ul>", "stimulus": "<p><b>Who Stole the Storybook?</b></p><p>When I was twelve, my grandmother's verandah in Mandeville was my library. Every Saturday I sat on the cool tiles with a book borrowed from the parish library and read until the smell of Sunday's rice and peas drifted through the window. Nobody forced me to read. I read because a story was the most exciting place I could go.</p><p>Today, I teach fifth-form English in Kingston, and that verandah feels like another century. When I asked my classes how many of them had finished a book for fun in the past year, only a handful of hands went up. A recent regional report on youth habits found that reading for pleasure has fallen sharply across the Caribbean, while time spent on screens has risen.</p><p>Why should this worry us? Because reading for pleasure does what no timetable can. It builds vocabulary without a single worksheet. It builds patience in a world of fifteen-second videos. It builds empathy, because every novel asks us to live, for a few hours, inside someone else's skin. Research suggests that young people who read for enjoyment tend to write more fluently and perform better across their subjects.</p><p>The phone is not the only culprit. Many teenagers tell me that the only books they meet are set texts, dissected line by line for examinations. We have taught them that reading is a chore to be survived, not a pleasure to be chosen.</p><p>The solution begins at home and in the classroom. Parents can leave books where phones usually lie. Schools can set aside fifteen minutes a day for silent reading of any book a student chooses, whether it is a collection of Anansi stories, a graphic novel or a cricket biography. Libraries can invite young people in rather than shushing them out.</p><p>Our grandmothers gave us verandahs and stories. Let us not hand our children only a screen and a silence.</p><p class=\"hint\">SOURCE: Marcia Whitter-Gayle (2025). \"Who Stole the Storybook?\" <i>The Island Courier</i>, Kingston, Jamaica (weekly opinion column). Author note: Marcia Whitter-Gayle has taught English at a Kingston high school for eighteen years and holds a master's degree in literacy education. She writes a weekly column on education.</p>", "title": "Analysing \"Who Stole the Storybook?\"", "essay": ["{{1|In \"Who Stole the Storybook?\", Marcia Whitter-Gayle, a Kingston English teacher with a master's degree in literacy education, examines why fewer Caribbean teenagers read for enjoyment.}} Her main message is that {{2|reading for pleasure is declining among Caribbean teenagers, and that parents, schools and libraries must make reading enjoyable again before its benefits are lost.}} {{3|Her purpose is to persuade Caribbean parents and educators that this decline is serious and to urge them to create time and space for free reading.}} {{4|She achieves this through a personal anecdote that sets up a contrast between past and present, and through deliberate repetition, while the information is only partly credible.}}", "Firstly, the writer opens with an anecdote about her childhood in Mandeville, where her grandmother's verandah \"was my library\" and she read \"until the smell of Sunday's rice and peas drifted through the window\". {{5|The sensory detail of a Jamaican Sunday conveys reading as warm, safe and pleasurable, so readers who share such memories feel nostalgia before any argument begins.}} The writer then contrasts this scene with her present classroom, where \"only a handful of hands went up\". {{6|Placed side by side, the two images emphasise how much has been lost in a single generation.}} This contrast supports her purpose because it turns a general trend into a personal loss that parents and teachers recognise and want to reverse.", "Furthermore, the writer uses repetition in the third paragraph. After the rhetorical question \"Why should this worry us?\", three sentences begin with the same words: \"It builds vocabulary\", \"It builds patience\" and \"It builds empathy\". {{7|The repeated structure creates a steady, persuasive rhythm, and each sentence adds a new benefit, so the case for reading appears to grow stronger with every line.}} {{8|The pattern also suggests that reading develops the whole person, not merely examination skills, which prepares readers for her later criticism of set texts \"dissected line by line for examinations\".}} In this way, the repetition makes the benefits memorable and gives educators a clear reason to act.", "{{9|The information is only partly credible.}} On one hand, the writer is well qualified: the author note states that she has taught English for \"eighteen years\" and holds a master's degree in literacy education, and the column was published in 2025, so it is current. On the other hand, her evidence is weak. {{10|The \"recent regional report\" is neither named nor dated, and the phrase \"Research suggests\" is not linked to any identifiable study.}} Her classroom question is also informal and limited to her own students, so it cannot represent all Caribbean teenagers. {{11|Because this is an opinion column, readers should accept her concern as informed but seek firmer data before treating it as proven.}}", "{{12|In conclusion, through a nostalgic anecdote and purposeful repetition, the writer persuasively urges adults to revive reading for pleasure, although her claims need stronger support.}}"], "notes": ["The opening names the writer, her credentials and the topic, giving the examiner immediate context.", "The main message is a complete idea (problem plus solution) well within the 30-word limit.", "A precise verb (persuade), a named audience and a clear 'what' make the purpose statement earn full credit.", "The thesis previews both techniques and the credibility judgement in the order the essay follows.", "Explain: the essay shows how the sensory detail works on the reader, not merely what it says.", "Naming the contrast explicitly turns two quotations into one analytical point.", "The essay explains the cumulative effect of the repetition rather than simply identifying it.", "A sharp link to a later part of the extract shows understanding of how the whole text is built.", "A clear verdict opens the credibility paragraph so the judgement is never left vague.", "Specific weaknesses are drawn from inside the extract: an unnamed, undated report and an uncited claim.", "The judgement considers genre, recognising that an opinion column is informed but not conclusive.", "The conclusion is shorter than the introduction and restates the argument without new analysis."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Content", "of": 10, "got": 10, "why": "A complete main message within 30 words, a precise purpose with audience, two techniques each quoted exactly and explained for their effect, and a credibility judgement with several reasons drawn from the author note, the date and the evidence inside the extract."}, {"part": "Organization", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "The thesis previews the order exactly, each body paragraph follows PEEL, transitions are clear (Firstly, Furthermore, On one hand, On the other hand) and the conclusion is shorter than the introduction."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 7, "why": "Formal, accurate Standard English with precise analytical verbs (conveys, emphasises, suggests). One mark held back: 'In this way, the repetition makes...' repeats the link idea already made; next step, vary link sentences so each adds something new."}], "upgrade": {"weak": "The writer uses repetition to make her point stronger. She says \"It builds\" a lot.", "strong": "The repeated opening \"It builds\" creates a persuasive rhythm in which each sentence adds a new benefit, so the case for reading seems to grow with every line and educators are given a clear reason to act.", "why": "The weak version only identifies the technique. The strong version quotes exactly, explains the effect on the reader and links it to the writer's purpose, which is what Grade One technique analysis requires."}}, {"prompt": "<p>Read the extract, then write an essay, in NO MORE than <b>500 words</b>, in which you</p><ul><li>state the writer's MAIN message in no more than 30 words</li><li>state the writer's purpose</li><li>discuss TWO organizational strategies OR TWO language techniques OR any combination of both which the writer uses to achieve the purpose</li><li>evaluate the credibility of the information presented.</li></ul>", "stimulus": "<p><b>Four Ships Before Breakfast</b></p><p>By eight o'clock on a Tuesday morning in high season, the horizon off Bridgetown is crowded with white towers. Four cruise ships have docked at the pier, and within an hour Broad Street is a river of sun hats and lanyards. Jewellers call out offers, taxi drivers wave laminated maps of Bathsheba and Harrison's Cave, and the smell of fish cakes drifts from a stall near the Careenage.</p><p>For many on the island, these mornings mean survival. \"On a four-ship day, I sell more coconut turnovers by noon than I sell all week in September,\" says Celestine Arrindell, who has run her food stall on the Careenage for eleven years. Taxi operators, tour guides and shop assistants tell similar stories. Cruise passengers, one port official explained, are the lifeblood of the Bridgetown economy.</p><p>Yet by mid-afternoon the picture changes. Traffic on Spring Garden Highway crawls as tour buses squeeze past residents trying to collect their children from school. Some residents complain that beaches near the capital become so crowded that locals no longer feel welcome. Environmental groups warn that heavy ship traffic may be harming the reefs and the water of Carlisle Bay, although they admit that more monitoring is needed.</p><p>Then the ships leave, and the street empties as quickly as it filled. On a ship-free Sunday, Broad Street is quiet, shutters are down, and vendors like Ms. Arrindell sit in the shade, waiting. The island has tied much of its fortune to a visitor who stays for only a few hours.</p><p>None of this means that cruise tourism should end. It means that Barbados must decide what kind of visitor economy it wants. More local products in the shops, better traffic planning on busy days and a fair share of earnings for the community would allow Bridgetown to welcome the world without losing itself.</p><p class=\"hint\">SOURCE: Damian Fleming-Rogers (2024). \"Four Ships Before Breakfast: Cruise Tourism and the Changing Face of Bridgetown.\" <i>Caribbean Horizons Magazine</i> (feature article). Author note: Damian Fleming-Rogers is a freelance travel writer who has covered Caribbean destinations for a decade. He spent several weeks in Bridgetown during the cruise season to report this feature.</p>", "title": "Analysing \"Four Ships Before Breakfast\"", "essay": ["{{1|In \"Four Ships Before Breakfast\", travel writer Damian Fleming-Rogers explores how cruise tourism shapes daily life in Bridgetown, Barbados.}} His main message is that {{2|cruise tourism sustains Bridgetown's economy but also brings congestion, crowding and dependence, so Barbados must manage it to benefit residents.}} {{3|His purpose is to inform magazine readers of both the benefits and the costs of cruise tourism and to encourage Barbados's leaders and residents to plan a fairer visitor economy.}} {{4|He achieves this through vivid imagery and a contrasting, time-based structure, and the information is moderately credible.}}", "Firstly, the writer uses vivid imagery to bring a cruise day to life. The ships are \"white towers\" on the horizon, and Broad Street becomes \"a river of sun hats and lanyards\". {{5|The river metaphor conveys the volume and constant movement of passengers, suggesting a force that fills the street and sweeps everything along.}} Sensory details, such as \"the smell of fish cakes\", root the scene in a recognisably Bajan setting. {{6|This imagery places readers on the street itself, so they grasp the scale of the industry before the writer weighs its costs, which supports his purpose of informing them fully.}}", "Furthermore, the writer organises the feature as a sequence of contrasting moments across a single day. The morning shows prosperity, illustrated by a vendor who sells \"more coconut turnovers by noon than I sell all week in September\". {{7|The transition \"Yet by mid-afternoon the picture changes\" signals a turn to traffic that \"crawls\" and crowded beaches, and finally to a \"ship-free Sunday\" when \"shutters are down\".}} {{8|This rise-and-fall pattern mirrors the island's dependence on visitors, emphasising that wealth arrives and leaves with the ships.}} {{9|By leading readers from boom to emptiness, the structure prepares them to accept his recommendations for \"better traffic planning\" and \"a fair share of earnings for the community\".}}", "{{10|The information is moderately credible.}} The writer gathered first-hand evidence by spending \"several weeks in Bridgetown during the cruise season\", and the feature, published in 2024, is reasonably current. He also quotes a named vendor with \"eleven years\" of experience and presents both benefits and drawbacks, which suggests balance. {{11|However, he is a travel writer rather than an economist, the \"port official\" is unnamed, and the environmental groups are unidentified and admit that \"more monitoring is needed\".}} Consequently, his observations are trustworthy, but his wider economic and environmental claims require verification.", "{{12|In conclusion, through vivid imagery and a contrasting structure, the writer informs readers of cruise tourism's double edge and encourages better planning, although some sources remain vague.}}"], "notes": ["The introduction identifies the writer, his role and the Barbados setting in one efficient sentence.", "The main message captures both sides of the feature (benefit and cost) and the solution in a single complete idea.", "The purpose combines two precise verbs (inform, encourage) with a clear audience, matching a balanced feature.", "The thesis names the two techniques in the order they are discussed and gives an early credibility verdict.", "Explain: the essay unpacks what the metaphor suggests about the crowd rather than simply labelling it.", "Link: the paragraph ends by connecting the imagery to the writer's purpose of informing readers.", "Several short, exact quotations trace the structure of the whole extract without wasting words.", "The essay explains why the structure matters: its shape mirrors the island's economic dependence.", "The organisational strategy is linked to the writer's recommendations, showing how structure serves purpose.", "A clear verdict opens the credibility paragraph, followed by reasons from the author note and source line.", "Weaknesses are drawn from inside the extract: an unnamed official and unidentified, uncertain groups.", "A brief conclusion restates the techniques and purpose with no new analysis."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Content", "of": 10, "got": 9, "why": "A complete main message, a precise two-part purpose, two well-explained techniques with exact quotations and a balanced credibility judgement. One mark held back: the imagery paragraph could explain more precisely how 'white towers' affects the reader; next step, analyse every quotation you include, or leave it out."}, {"part": "Organization", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "The thesis maps the essay, both body paragraphs follow PEEL in the promised order, the credibility paragraph moves logically from strengths to weaknesses, and the conclusion is shorter than the introduction."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 7, "why": "Formal, varied and accurate, with effective analytical verbs (conveys, suggests, emphasises). One mark held back: 'Consequently' in the credibility paragraph does not quite fit a balanced judgement; next step, choose transitions that match the logic exactly, such as 'Overall'."}], "upgrade": {"weak": "The information is credible because the writer went to Bridgetown and talked to people.", "strong": "The information is moderately credible: the writer spent several weeks in Bridgetown and quotes a named vendor, but the port official is unnamed and the environmental groups admit that more monitoring is needed.", "why": "The weak version gives one vague reason. The strong version gives a clear verdict and weighs specific strengths against specific weaknesses from the author note and the extract."}}, {"prompt": "<p>Read the extract, then write an essay, in NO MORE than <b>500 words</b>, in which you</p><ul><li>state the writer's MAIN message in no more than 30 words</li><li>state the writer's purpose</li><li>discuss TWO organizational strategies OR TWO language techniques OR any combination of both which the writer uses to achieve the purpose</li><li>evaluate the credibility of the information presented.</li></ul>", "stimulus": "<p><b>Pour Out the Habit</b></p><p>Good evening, parents, teachers and members of the Parent-Teacher Association. Thank you for leaving your homes on a weeknight to join us here in San Fernando.</p><p>Let me begin with a boy I will call Jaden. He was nine years old when his mother brought him to my clinic, tired all the time and struggling to climb the stairs at school. When I asked what he drank each day, she said proudly, \"Only juice and soft drinks, Doctor. He doesn't like water.\" Jaden was not a greedy child. He was a thirsty child who had been taught that thirst tastes sweet.</p><p>Jaden is not alone. In clinics across Trinidad and Tobago, we are seeing more children who are overweight, and more young people developing conditions that we once saw mainly in their grandparents. Diabetes and heart disease already touch almost every family in this room. Studies show that sugary drinks are one of the largest sources of added sugar in children's diets.</p><p>Ask yourself: what is in your child's lunch kit tomorrow? Is it a bottle of brightly coloured drink? Is it a box of sweetened juice? Many of these drinks contain more sugar in a single serving than a child should have in a whole day.</p><p>I am not asking you to give up our culture. Sorrel at Christmas and a cold mauby on a hot afternoon are part of who we are. I am asking you to make sweet drinks a treat, not a habit. Fill the lunch kit with water. Buy a coconut from the vendor instead of a soft drink. Let your children see you choose water first.</p><p>Parents, you are the most powerful health officers in this country. Tonight, I ask you to pour out the habit, not just the drink. Our children's future depends on it.</p><p class=\"hint\">SOURCE: Dr. Anjali Rampersad-Clarke. \"Pour Out the Habit.\" Address to a Parent-Teacher Association health forum, San Fernando, Trinidad. Transcript circulated by the forum organisers (undated). Author note: Dr. Anjali Rampersad-Clarke is a paediatrician with more than fifteen years of clinical practice and serves as a senior public health officer.</p>", "title": "Analysing \"Pour Out the Habit\"", "essay": ["{{1|In \"Pour Out the Habit\", paediatrician and senior public health officer Dr. Anjali Rampersad-Clarke addresses parents at a health forum in San Fernando, Trinidad.}} Her main message is that {{2|sugary drinks are damaging the health of Trinidad and Tobago's children, so parents must make them an occasional treat and offer water instead.}} {{3|Her purpose is to persuade parents that everyday sweet drinks endanger their children and to urge them to change family habits immediately.}} {{4|She achieves this through an emotive anecdote and a series of rhetorical questions in direct address, and the information is largely, but not fully, credible.}}", "Firstly, the speaker opens with an anecdote about a nine-year-old patient she calls Jaden, who was \"tired all the time and struggling to climb the stairs at school\". {{5|This concrete image of a child too weak for ordinary activity appeals to parents' protective instincts far more powerfully than a general warning could.}} The mother's proud reply, \"Only juice and soft drinks, Doctor\", {{6|suggests that harmful habits often begin with good intentions, so listeners recognise themselves without feeling accused.}} The line \"He was a thirsty child who had been taught that thirst tastes sweet\" shifts responsibility from the child to the adults around him. {{7|The anecdote therefore makes the danger personal and prepares parents to accept that they have the power to change it.}}", "Furthermore, the speaker uses rhetorical questions and direct address to involve her audience. She instructs listeners to \"Ask yourself\" before posing the questions \"what is in your child's lunch kit tomorrow?\" and \"Is it a bottle of brightly coloured drink?\" {{8|These questions move the issue from her clinic into each listener's kitchen, compelling parents to examine their own choices silently.}} {{9|Because the questions are followed by the claim that such drinks may contain \"more sugar in a single serving than a child should have in a whole day\", the honest answer feels uncomfortable and urgent.}} This technique builds directly towards her call to action, \"Fill the lunch kit with water\", fulfilling her purpose of urging immediate change.", "{{10|The information is largely credible.}} The speaker is a paediatrician with \"more than fifteen years of clinical practice\", and her observations come from her own clinic, so she speaks with professional authority. Her respect for \"Sorrel at Christmas\" also suggests a balanced, culturally aware view rather than an extreme one. {{11|However, the transcript is undated, \"Jaden\" is a pseudonym whose case cannot be checked, and the statement \"Studies show\" names no study.}} A single anecdote cannot prove a national trend, so parents should treat her warning as expert advice that cited research would strengthen.", "{{12|In conclusion, through an emotive anecdote and probing rhetorical questions, the speaker persuasively urges parents to replace sugary drinks with water, despite gaps in her evidence.}}"], "notes": ["The opening establishes the speaker's expertise, the audience and the Trinidad setting, which matter for a speech.", "The main message is a complete idea, naming both the problem and the action, in under 30 words.", "A precise purpose: two verbs (persuade, urge), a clear audience and exactly what they should do.", "The thesis previews both techniques and the credibility verdict in the order the essay follows.", "Explain: the essay identifies the emotional appeal and why it works on this audience of parents.", "Analysis of tone: the essay notices that the anecdote avoids blaming parents, a subtle persuasive choice.", "Link: the paragraph closes by connecting the anecdote to the purpose of prompting change.", "The effect of the questions is explained in terms of what they make the listener do.", "The essay shows how one technique works together with a supporting claim to create urgency.", "A clear credibility verdict, supported first by the speaker's qualifications from the author note.", "Three precise weaknesses: the missing date, an unverifiable case and an uncited source inside the extract.", "The conclusion is shorter than the introduction and adds no new analysis."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Content", "of": 10, "got": 10, "why": "All four parts of the question are fully addressed: a concise main message, a precise purpose with audience, two techniques each named, quoted exactly and linked to purpose, and a credibility judgement with strengths and several specific weaknesses."}, {"part": "Organization", "of": 7, "got": 6, "why": "Clear thesis, PEEL paragraphs in the promised order and a short conclusion. One mark held back: the second body paragraph drifts into the call to action, which was not previewed in the thesis; next step, keep each paragraph to the technique you named or add it to your thesis."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 8, "why": "Fluent, formal Standard English with varied sentence structures and precise analytical vocabulary (appeals to, suggests, compelling), free of errors."}], "upgrade": {"weak": "Her purpose is to persuade.", "strong": "Her purpose is to persuade parents that everyday sweet drinks endanger their children and to urge them to change family habits immediately.", "why": "A purpose verb on its own earns nothing. The strong version adds the audience (parents), what they should believe and what they should do, which is what examiners reward."}}, {"prompt": "<p>Read the extract, then write an essay, in NO MORE than <b>500 words</b>, in which you</p><ul><li>state the writer's MAIN message in no more than 30 words</li><li>state the writer's purpose</li><li>discuss TWO organizational strategies OR TWO language techniques OR any combination of both which the writer uses to achieve the purpose</li><li>evaluate the credibility of the information presented.</li></ul>", "stimulus": "<p><b>The Storm in Our Phones</b></p><p>Last hurricane season, a voice note began circulating on WhatsApp just after midnight. A calm male voice announced that a powerful hurricane would strike Antigua within twelve hours and that the airport would close at dawn. By sunrise, the queue at one St. John's supermarket stretched into the car park, shelves of bottled water had been stripped bare, and frightened elderly residents in Liberta and Parham were calling relatives in tears.</p><p>There was no hurricane. There was not even a tropical storm. The weather system in question was a harmless tropical wave far out in the Atlantic, as the Antigua and Barbuda Meteorological Service confirmed the next morning.</p><p>Fake warnings like this spread faster than any storm. They travel from phone to phone, from family group to church group, gathering credibility simply because they arrive from someone we trust. A forwarded message from an aunt feels more believable than an official bulletin, even when the aunt has no idea where it came from.</p><p>The damage is real. Panic buying leaves the most vulnerable without supplies. Families waste money they cannot spare. Worst of all, every false alarm teaches people to ignore the next warning, and one day the next warning will be true.</p><p>Who benefits from this confusion? No one. Who pays for it? All of us.</p><p>This newspaper therefore calls on every reader to adopt three simple habits this hurricane season. Before you forward, check the official Meteorological Service channels. If a message does not name its source, do not share it. When you see a rumour in your family chat, correct it kindly with the official information. Hurricanes are a fact of Caribbean life. Panic should not be.</p><p class=\"hint\">SOURCE: Editorial (2024). \"The Storm in Our Phones.\" <i>The Leeward Sentinel</i>, St. John's, Antigua and Barbuda. Author note: Editorials in <i>The Leeward Sentinel</i> (a newspaper) are written by its editorial board and are unsigned. The newspaper has reported on national affairs for more than thirty years.</p>", "title": "Analysing \"The Storm in Our Phones\"", "essay": ["{{1|In the editorial \"The Storm in Our Phones\", published in 2024 by The Leeward Sentinel, the newspaper's editorial board addresses the spread of false hurricane warnings on WhatsApp in Antigua and Barbuda.}} Its main message is that {{2|fake hurricane warnings shared on WhatsApp cause real panic and harm, so citizens must verify messages with official sources before forwarding them.}} {{3|Its purpose is to warn readers about the damage caused by fake warnings and to persuade them to adopt responsible sharing habits this hurricane season.}} {{4|The writers achieve this through an opening anecdote with a sudden reversal and an extended storm comparison, and the information is partly credible.}}", "Firstly, the editorial is organised around an opening anecdote that ends in a dramatic reversal. It describes a midnight voice note that caused a supermarket queue to stretch \"into the car park\" and left elderly residents \"calling relatives in tears\". {{5|These details build tension, so readers experience the same rising fear as the residents.}} The writers then break the tension with two short sentences: \"There was no hurricane. There was not even a tropical storm.\" {{6|The abrupt shift from long, crowded description to blunt statements emphasises how needless the panic was.}} {{7|This structure leaves readers frustrated on the residents' behalf, preparing them to accept the later instruction to \"check the official Meteorological Service channels\".}}", "Furthermore, the editorial develops a comparison between rumours and storms, introduced in its title. It states that fake warnings \"spread faster than any storm\" and travel \"from family group to church group, gathering credibility\". {{8|Presenting rumours as a storm suggests that misinformation is itself a hazard, as destructive as the weather it pretends to report.}} {{9|For a Caribbean audience familiar with hurricane preparation, the comparison implies that people should guard against false messages as carefully as they prepare for wind and rain.}} The closing lines, \"Hurricanes are a fact of Caribbean life. Panic should not be\", reinforce this link and support the purpose of persuading readers to change their habits.", "{{10|The information is partly credible.}} The newspaper has reported on national affairs \"for more than thirty years\", the editorial is dated 2024, and its key fact is attributed to the Antigua and Barbuda Meteorological Service, a recognised national authority. {{11|However, the editorial is unsigned, so no individual writer's expertise can be judged, and the incident is dated only as \"Last hurricane season\", with the supermarket and residents unnamed.}} The Meteorological Service is also cited without a named spokesperson or bulletin. Therefore, the editorial's advice is sound, but its central anecdote cannot be independently verified.", "{{12|In conclusion, through a reversing anecdote and a sustained storm comparison, the editorial effectively warns readers against fake warnings, although its evidence is partly unverifiable.}}"], "notes": ["The opening uses the source line to identify the genre, the publication, the date and the topic at once.", "The main message is a complete idea linking the problem to the required action, in under 30 words.", "The purpose pairs two precise verbs (warn, persuade) with the audience and the specific habit to adopt.", "The thesis previews one organisational strategy and one language technique, then the credibility verdict.", "Explain: the essay describes the effect of the details on the reader's emotions, not just their content.", "The essay analyses sentence length as part of the structure, a detail that lifts the analysis.", "Link: the strategy is tied to the editorial's practical advice, showing how structure serves purpose.", "The comparison is interpreted: the essay states what it suggests about misinformation.", "Audience awareness: the essay explains why the comparison works especially well for Caribbean readers.", "A clear verdict opens the credibility paragraph, supported by the source line and the named institution.", "Weaknesses come from the author note (unsigned) and the extract itself (a vague date and unnamed people).", "The conclusion is shorter than the introduction and summarises without adding new analysis."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Content", "of": 10, "got": 9, "why": "A precise main message and purpose, one organisational strategy and one language technique, each quoted exactly and explained, and a well-reasoned credibility judgement. One mark held back: the credibility paragraph could note that an editorial is opinion by nature; next step, always consider genre when judging credibility."}, {"part": "Organization", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "The introduction moves from context to main message, purpose and thesis; both body paragraphs follow PEEL in the previewed order; transitions are clear; the conclusion is brief and adds nothing new."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 7, "why": "Formal and accurate, with strong analytical verbs (emphasises, suggests, implies, reinforces). One mark held back: the long opening sentence is slightly overloaded; next step, split context and publication details into two sentences for clarity."}], "upgrade": {"weak": "The writer uses a comparison when he says the fake warnings are like a storm, which is a good comparison.", "strong": "By stating that fake warnings \"spread faster than any storm\", the editorial suggests that misinformation is itself a hazard, so Caribbean readers are urged to guard against rumours as carefully as they prepare for hurricanes.", "why": "The weak version guesses the writer's gender for an unsigned editorial, paraphrases instead of quoting and merely praises the technique. The strong version quotes exactly, interprets the effect and links it to the audience and purpose."}}], "m2": [{"prompt": "<p>Read the stimulus, then write an essay, in NO MORE than <b>500 words</b>, in which you</p><ul><li>state ONE reason each for the host's use of TWO language varieties in the transcript</li><li>identify, with examples from the transcript, ONE grammatical feature of Miss Pearline's Creole</li><li>identify, with examples from the transcript, ONE phonological feature of Miss Pearline's Creole</li><li>identify and explain ONE attitude to Creole revealed in the exchange.</li></ul>", "stimulus": "<p><i>The following exchange took place on a morning call-in programme on a Kingston radio station. The host, Desmond Clarke, is discussing the water restrictions announced during the dry season.</i></p><p><b>HOST:</b> Good morning, Jamaica, and welcome back. Today we are discussing the water restrictions announced for parts of Kingston and St Andrew. Our lines are open. Caller, you are on the air.</p><p><b>MISS PEARLINE:</b> Mawnin, Missa Clarke. A Miss Pearline from August Town.</p><p><b>HOST:</b> Mawnin, Miss Pearline! Wah gwaan? Tell mi wha happen up your side.</p><p><b>MISS PEARLINE:</b> Missa Clarke, mi nuh know wah fi do. Di pipe dem dry from Monday. Wi cyaan wash, wi cyaan cook. Mi did haffi go fetch water from di standpipe wid two bucket.</p><p><b>HOST:</b> Lawd, Miss Pearline, dat rough. Two bucket inna dis heat?</p><p><b>MISS PEARLINE:</b> Every day! An mi seventy-two years old. Nobody nuh come tell wi nutten.</p><p><b>HOST:</b> Miss Pearline, I hear you. [pause] For the benefit of our listeners, the Commission has said that trucks will be sent to affected communities. Residents should call the customer service line to report outages.</p><p><b>MISS PEARLINE:</b> Truck? Mi nuh see no truck. Dem fi come look pon wi.</p><p><b>HOST:</b> Well, we are going to put that question to the Commission when their representative joins us at nine o'clock.</p><p><b>MISS PEARLINE:</b> Tank yuh, Missa Clarke. Yuh a di only one weh listen to wi.</p><p><b>HOST:</b> Walk good, Miss Pearline. [to the audience] And that, ladies and gentlemen, is the reality for many of our elderly residents. Next caller, you are on the air.</p><p><b>ANDRE:</b> Good morning. Mr Clarke, with respect, why do you allow callers to speak like that on air? Patois is fine at home, but national radio should set a standard.</p><p><b>HOST:</b> Andre, Miss Pearline spoke in the language she thinks and feels in. If I demanded \"proper English\", half of Jamaica would never call this programme.</p>", "title": "The radio call-in", "essay": ["{{1|In this transcript from a Kingston radio call-in programme, the host, Desmond Clarke, discusses dry-season water restrictions with two callers: Miss Pearline, an elderly resident of August Town, and Andre, who objects to her language.}} The exchange shows that the host moves skilfully between Standard English and Jamaican Creole to serve both his callers and his national audience. {{2|This essay will explain one reason for the host's use of each variety, identify one grammatical and one phonological feature of Miss Pearline's Creole, and examine the negative attitude to Creole that Andre reveals.}}", "{{3|Firstly, the host uses Standard English to fulfil his public, professional role as a broadcaster.}} When he reports that \"trucks will be sent to affected communities\", he is relaying official information to thousands of listeners. Standard English is associated with formality and authority in Jamaica, so it lends that information credibility and signals that he is speaking as the host, not merely as a sympathetic neighbour.", "{{4|In contrast, he switches to Jamaican Creole to build rapport with Miss Pearline.}} As soon as she greets him in Creole, he replies, \"Mawnin, Miss Pearline! Wah gwaan?\", matching her variety. {{5|This is convergence: by accommodating to the caller's speech, he puts an elderly, distressed woman at ease and signals that her problem matters to him.}} Her reply, \"Yuh a di only one weh listen to wi\", suggests that the strategy succeeds.", "{{6|One grammatical feature of Miss Pearline's Creole is multiple negation, in which more than one negative word is used in a single clause.}} {{7|She complains, \"Nobody nuh come tell wi nutten\", where Standard English requires 'Nobody came to tell us anything'.}} Similarly, \"Mi nuh see no truck\" contains two negatives, whereas Standard English uses only one: 'I have not seen any truck'. In Creole, the extra negatives intensify rather than cancel the meaning.", "{{8|A phonological feature of her speech is th-stopping, in which the dental fricatives /θ/ and /ð/ of Standard English are pronounced as the stops /t/ and /d/.}} This is evident in \"Tank yuh\" for 'thank you', \"Di pipe dem\" for 'the pipes' and \"nutten\" for 'nothing'. Because the substitution is consistent, it is a systematic sound rule of the Creole rather than careless speech.", "{{9|Finally, the exchange reveals a negative attitude to Creole, expressed by the second caller, Andre.}} He asks why the host allows callers \"to speak like that on air\" and insists that \"Patois is fine at home, but national radio should set a standard.\" {{10|This attitude confines Creole to private, informal domains and reserves public life for Standard English, a view rooted in the colonial association of English with education and status.}} The host challenges this view, implying that excluding Creole would silence many citizens.", "{{11|In conclusion, Desmond Clarke uses Standard English to inform and Creole to connect, while Miss Pearline's speech displays multiple negation and th-stopping.}} Andre's objection shows that, despite its wide use, Creole remains undervalued in public spaces."], "notes": ["The introduction establishes context (who, where, what) in a single precise sentence, naming every speaker the essay will discuss.", "The thesis previews all four bullets in the order the body paragraphs follow, giving the examiner a clear map.", "Point: the topic sentence names the variety and its reason, tying it to the host's role rather than giving a general fact about Standard English.", "The contrast transition signals the second variety, and the reason (rapport) is specific to this caller.", "Explain: the technical term 'convergence' is used accurately and linked to the effect on the caller, which lifts the analysis above description.", "The grammatical feature is named and defined before any example is given, so the examiner can award the mark immediately.", "An exact quotation from the stimulus is paired with its Standard English equivalent, which is what Grade One answers consistently do.", "The phonological feature is named with the correct sounds, keeping grammar and phonology clearly separate.", "The attitude is identified explicitly as negative and attributed to a named speaker.", "Explain and Link: the attitude is traced to its historical cause, showing understanding rather than mere identification.", "The conclusion is shorter than the introduction and restates each bullet briefly without adding new analysis."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Content", "of": 10, "got": 10, "why": "Every bullet is answered: two varieties with a stimulus-specific reason each, multiple negation and th-stopping correctly named with exact quotations and Standard English equivalents, and a negative attitude identified and explained."}, {"part": "Organization", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "A thesis that previews the bullets in order, one PEEL paragraph per bullet, clear transitions (Firstly, In contrast, Finally) and a concise conclusion."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 7, "why": "Accurate, formal Standard English with precise terminology. One mark held back: the closing sentence of the phonology paragraph is slightly general; tie it back to Miss Pearline's identity or the radio context instead."}], "upgrade": {"weak": "The host speaks Creole because he is Jamaican and that is how Jamaicans talk.", "strong": "The host switches to Jamaican Creole to build rapport with Miss Pearline, converging to her variety with \"Mawnin, Miss Pearline! Wah gwaan?\" so that an elderly, distressed caller feels at ease.", "why": "The weak version gives a reason that would apply to any Jamaican in any situation. The strong version names a purpose, supports it with an exact quotation and explains its effect in this particular exchange."}}, {"prompt": "<p>Read the stimulus, then write an essay, in NO MORE than <b>500 words</b>, in which you</p><ul><li>explain how the applicant's language choices may affect the interviewer's perception of him, with reference to language attitudes</li><li>identify, with examples from the transcript, ONE grammatical feature of the applicant's Creole</li><li>discuss the register appropriate to this context and explain why it is appropriate.</li></ul>", "stimulus": "<p><i>The following is an extract from a job interview at the head office of a retail company in Port of Spain, Trinidad. Mrs Lorraine Charles, the Human Resources Manager, is interviewing nineteen-year-old Kevon Mohammed for the position of customer service representative.</i></p><p><b>MRS CHARLES:</b> Good afternoon, Mr Mohammed. Please have a seat. Could you begin by telling me a little about yourself?</p><p><b>KEVON:</b> Good afternoon, Miss. I mean, Mrs Charles. I completed my CAPE examinations last year at a school in Chaguanas, and I have been working part-time at my uncle's hardware.</p><p><b>MRS CHARLES:</b> Good. What did your duties at the hardware involve?</p><p><b>KEVON:</b> Well, I does handle the cash register and I does help customers find what they looking for. Sometimes it have real long lines on a Saturday, so you have to keep calm.</p><p><b>MRS CHARLES:</b> I see. Tell me about a time you dealt with a difficult customer.</p><p><b>KEVON:</b> [laughs nervously] Oh gosh. It had this man one time, he come in vex because we eh had the cement he want. He start to cuss up the whole place. I tell him, \"Sir, doh worry, we go get it Monday.\" And he calm down.</p><p><b>MRS CHARLES:</b> How did you resolve the situation in the end?</p><p><b>KEVON:</b> Sorry, sorry. I explained that a delivery was expected on Monday, and I offered to telephone him when it arrived. He returned on Monday and purchased the cement.</p><p><b>MRS CHARLES:</b> That is a good outcome. Why would you like to work with our company?</p><p><b>KEVON:</b> Because allyuh have a good name, and I does like dealing with people. I eh go lie, Miss, I real nervous right now.</p><p><b>MRS CHARLES:</b> [smiling] That is quite all right, Mr Mohammed. Take your time.</p>", "title": "The job interview", "essay": ["{{1|This extract records part of a job interview in Port of Spain in which Mrs Lorraine Charles, a Human Resources Manager, interviews nineteen-year-old Kevon Mohammed for a customer service post.}} Although Kevon begins in Standard English, he slips into Trinidadian Creole whenever his nerves take over. {{2|This essay will explain how his language choices may affect the interviewer's perception of him, identify one grammatical feature of his Creole, and discuss the register appropriate to the interview and why.}}", "{{3|Firstly, Kevon's shifts into Creole may shape Mrs Charles's perception of him because of widely held language attitudes.}} In many Caribbean workplaces, Standard English is associated with education and professionalism, while Creole is often linked, unfairly, with informality or a lack of competence. When Kevon describes a customer who came in \"vex\" and began \"to cuss up the whole place\", an interviewer who holds these attitudes may doubt whether he could address the company's clients appropriately. {{4|However, his choices may also work in his favour.}} When asked to explain again, he reformulates his answer at once: \"I explained that a delivery was expected on Monday, and I offered to telephone him when it arrived.\" {{5|This self-correction shows that he commands both varieties and can switch to the standard when required, which is precisely the skill a customer service representative needs.}} His frank admission of nervousness may also make him appear sincere, as Mrs Charles's smile suggests.", "{{6|One grammatical feature of Kevon's Creole is the use of \"does\" as a pre-verbal habitual aspect marker, which shows that an action happens regularly.}} {{7|He states, \"I does handle the cash register\", where Standard English requires 'I handle the cash register'.}} The same marker appears in \"I does help customers\" and \"I does like dealing with people\". Standard English expresses habit through the simple present tense alone, so in a formal interview this marker immediately identifies his speech as Creole.", "{{8|The register appropriate to this context is the formal register, expressed in Standard English.}} A job interview is a transactional exchange between strangers of unequal status: Mrs Charles holds the power to hire, and Kevon must present himself favourably. Mrs Charles models this register herself through polite, complete questions such as \"Could you begin by telling me a little about yourself?\" {{9|Moreover, because the post involves representing the company to the public, the interview is effectively a test of the language Kevon would use with customers.}} Formal choices such as \"explained\", \"expected\" and \"purchased\" in his corrected answer meet this expectation, whereas casual expressions such as \"Oh gosh\" and \"I eh go lie\", together with addressing her as \"Miss\", suggest a familiarity that the setting does not allow.", "{{10|In conclusion, Kevon's slips into Creole may lead Mrs Charles to judge him negatively, although his self-correction reveals genuine linguistic competence.}} His habitual \"does\" is typical of Trinidadian Creole, but the formal register remains the wisest choice in any job interview."], "notes": ["The context sentence identifies the speakers, their roles and the setting, which the later analysis of status depends on.", "The thesis previews the three bullets in the exact order of the question.", "Point: the topic sentence ties Kevon's language directly to the key term 'language attitudes'.", "A contrasting transition introduces a second side, showing that perception is not automatically negative.", "Explain: the writer links the evidence to the demands of the specific job, which makes the reason specific to the stimulus.", "The feature is named precisely (pre-verbal habitual aspect marker) and defined before examples are given.", "An exact quotation is followed by its Standard English equivalent, demonstrating the difference clearly.", "The register is named and the paragraph then explains why it fits, answering both parts of the bullet.", "Link: the writer connects the interview register to the job itself, a sophisticated, context-specific reason.", "The conclusion restates all three bullets briefly and adds no new evidence."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Content", "of": 10, "got": 9, "why": "All three bullets are answered with exact quotations, and the attitudes paragraph balances negative and positive perceptions. One mark held back: the register paragraph could briefly name the features of the formal register (complete sentences, titles, no slang) as a list before applying them."}, {"part": "Organization", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "Clear thesis, one paragraph per bullet in the promised order, effective transitions (Firstly, However, Moreover) and a short conclusion."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 7, "why": "Controlled, formal Standard English with varied sentences. One mark held back: the attitudes paragraph is long; splitting the counter-point into its own paragraph would improve readability."}], "upgrade": {"weak": "Kevon uses bad English, so he will not get the job.", "strong": "Because Creole is often linked, unfairly, with informality, an interviewer who hears Kevon say \"I does handle the cash register\" may doubt his professionalism; however, his immediate switch to Standard English shows that he controls both varieties.", "why": "The weak version labels Creole as 'bad English', which is itself a language attitude, and predicts an outcome without evidence. The strong version names the attitude, quotes the stimulus and offers a balanced judgement."}}, {"prompt": "<p>Read the stimulus, then write an essay, in NO MORE than <b>500 words</b>, in which you</p><ul><li>explain the role of language in preserving cultural identity, as illustrated in the extract</li><li>identify, with examples from the extract, ONE grammatical feature of Sint Maarten English</li><li>discuss TWO ways in which technology could help or threaten this storytelling tradition.</li></ul>", "stimulus": "<p><i>The following extract is adapted from a community feature in a Sint Maarten newspaper.</i></p><p><b>Granny Ione Keeps the Old Stories Alive</b></p><p>Every Saturday afternoon, under the tamarind tree in her yard in Middle Region, eighty-one-year-old Ione Richardson gathers a dozen children for what she calls \"story time the old way\". There are no tablets and no television: only a long wooden bench, a plate of johnny cakes and Mrs Richardson's voice.</p><p>She tells the stories in Sint Maarten English, the way her own grandmother told them to her. \"When me was a lil girl, we ain' had no TV,\" she explained. \"Granny does sit we down on the step and tell we 'bout Nancy and the jumbie dem. Now is my turn.\"</p><p>In one favourite tale, Nancy tricks Tiger out of a basket of guavaberries. The children shout the warning back to her in chorus: \"Tiger, watch youself!\"</p><p>Ten-year-old Mateo, whose parents moved to the island from the Dominican Republic, admitted that he did not understand everything at first. \"Now I know what 'jumbie' mean,\" he said proudly.</p><p>Mrs Richardson worries that the old words are fading. \"De chirren dem talking like what they see on the phone. If we don' use we own words, dey going lose dem.\"</p><p>Her granddaughter, a teacher, has begun recording the sessions on her phone. She hopes to share the recordings with families overseas and with schools before Sint Maarten Day in November.</p>", "title": "The storyteller of Middle Region", "essay": ["{{1|This newspaper extract profiles Ione Richardson, an eighty-one-year-old grandmother in Middle Region, Sint Maarten, who tells traditional folk stories to children every Saturday in Sint Maarten English.}} The article presents her storytelling as a living link between generations. {{2|This essay will explain the role of language in preserving cultural identity, identify one grammatical feature of Sint Maarten English, and discuss one way in which technology could help and one way in which it could threaten this tradition.}}", "{{3|Firstly, language preserves cultural identity because it carries a community's knowledge, values and humour from one generation to the next.}} Mrs Richardson tells the stories \"the way her own grandmother told them to her\", so the variety itself is part of the inheritance. Words such as \"jumbie\" belong to the island's folk beliefs; when Mateo says, \"Now I know what 'jumbie' mean\", he is not merely learning a word but entering a shared cultural world. {{4|This is significant because Mateo's parents came from the Dominican Republic: Sint Maarten English is helping a child of migrant parents to claim the island's heritage as his own.}} Mrs Richardson's warning, \"If we don' use we own words, dey going lose dem\", shows her awareness that when a variety fades, the identity it carries may fade with it.", "{{5|One grammatical feature of Sint Maarten English is the invariable pronoun \"we\", used in object and possessive positions where Standard English changes the form of the pronoun.}} {{6|Mrs Richardson recalls, \"Granny does sit we down on the step and tell we 'bout Nancy\", where Standard English requires 'sit us down' and 'tell us'.}} Similarly, \"we own words\" means 'our own words'. Whereas Standard English marks case through three forms, Sint Maarten English relies on word order alone to show the pronoun's function.", "{{7|Secondly, technology could help this tradition by recording the stories and spreading them beyond the yard.}} Mrs Richardson's granddaughter is recording the sessions and hopes to share them \"with families overseas and with schools\". Such recordings would preserve her voice and vocabulary long after she can no longer perform, allowing St. Maarteners abroad and students to hear Sint Maarten English used with pride. {{8|In this way, a single phone could turn a gathering of a dozen children into an archive for the whole community.}}", "{{9|On the other hand, technology could threaten the tradition by replacing face-to-face storytelling with imported entertainment.}} Mrs Richardson observes, \"De chirren dem talking like what they see on the phone.\" Children who spend hours on foreign videos may adopt overseas accents and expressions while losing local words and the habit of gathering to listen. {{10|Consequently, the very device that can record the stories may also draw children away from the tamarind tree where those stories are told.}}", "{{11|In conclusion, Mrs Richardson's stories show that Sint Maarten English, with features such as the invariable pronoun \"we\", carries the island's cultural identity.}} Technology may either preserve that voice or drown it out, depending on how the community chooses to use it."], "notes": ["The opening sentence gives full context (who, where, what and in which variety) so the examiner knows the stimulus has been understood.", "The thesis previews the bullets in order and commits to one 'help' and one 'threaten', making the TWO ways explicit.", "Point: the topic sentence states a general principle that the paragraph then proves from the extract.", "Explain: the writer draws an insight from a detail in the stimulus (Mateo's background) that many candidates would overlook.", "The feature is named precisely and defined in terms of grammatical function, not just described as 'wrong English'.", "An exact quotation is paired with Standard English equivalents, satisfying the 'with examples' instruction fully.", "The first technology point is clearly signposted and labelled as a way technology could help.", "Link: a memorable closing sentence ties the evidence back to the community, completing the PEEL structure.", "The contrast transition signals the opposing way, giving the answer balance.", "Consequently introduces a clear cause-and-effect conclusion to the paragraph, using an image from the stimulus.", "The conclusion is brief, restates the bullets and adds no new analysis."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Content", "of": 10, "got": 10, "why": "The role of language in identity is explained with stimulus-specific evidence, the invariable pronoun 'we' is correctly named with exact examples and Standard English equivalents, and two distinct technology points (one help, one threat) are fully developed."}, {"part": "Organization", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "A thesis that maps the essay, one PEEL paragraph per point in the promised order, strong transitions and a concise conclusion."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 7, "why": "Fluent, formal Standard English with precise vocabulary. One mark held back: the final sentence of the grammar paragraph is compressed; spell out the three Standard English forms ('we', 'us', 'our') to make the contrast unmistakable."}], "upgrade": {"weak": "Technology is good because people can watch the stories on YouTube.", "strong": "Technology could help the tradition because recordings, such as those Mrs Richardson's granddaughter is making, would preserve her voice and vocabulary and carry Sint Maarten English to \"families overseas and with schools\".", "why": "The weak version names a platform but does not explain how the tradition or the language is preserved. The strong version links the technology to a detail in the extract and states its effect on cultural identity."}}, {"prompt": "<p>Read the stimulus, then write an essay, in NO MORE than <b>500 words</b>, in which you</p><ul><li>identify and explain TWO features of the language of electronic communication, with examples from the transcript</li><li>identify, with examples from the transcript, ONE grammatical feature of Guyanese Creole</li><li>explain ONE effect of electronic media on the use of Standard English.</li></ul>", "stimulus": "<p><i>The following messages were posted on a Saturday evening in a WhatsApp group called \"Seawall Squad\", created by four secondary school students in Georgetown, Guyana, who are planning a Sunday afternoon outing to the seawall.</i></p><p><b>ANIKA:</b> gm... i mean gn 😂 so wah, allyuh still going seawall tmrw??</p><p><b>RAVI:</b> yesss bhai 💯 me deh ready from now</p><p><b>SHONETTE:</b> wat time tho? me mudda seh me gotta reach home b4 dark 😩</p><p><b>ANIKA:</b> 4:30 by the bandstand? we could ketch the sunset 🌅</p><p><b>DAMION:</b> bet. who bringing de coconut water</p><p><b>RAVI:</b> me gon walk wid two bottle. me granny bin mek pholourie tday so me gon bring some 2 😋</p><p><b>SHONETTE:</b> RAVI U IS DE GOAT 🐐🐐</p><p><b>DAMION:</b> lol she pholourie does sell out quick quick at de church fair</p><p><b>ANIKA:</b> ok ok focus 😤 de minibus does full up Sunday so leff early</p><p><b>DAMION:</b> ngl me nah able wid dat walk from Kitty 🥵</p><p><b>SHONETTE:</b> stop complain bai 🙄 is exercise</p><p><b>RAVI:</b> 😂😂😂 she right tho, de sea breeze does be nice</p><p><b>ANIKA:</b> also pls nobody post no pics of me on IG till i fix up meself</p><p><b>DAMION:</b> no promises 📸</p><p><b>SHONETTE:</b> ok so 4:30 bandstand. dont be late Damion!!!</p>", "title": "The seawall group chat", "essay": ["{{1|This transcript records a WhatsApp group chat in which four secondary school students in Georgetown, Guyana, plan a Sunday afternoon outing to the seawall.}} Their messages blend Guyanese Creole with the conventions of electronic communication. {{2|This essay will identify two features of the language of electronic communication, identify one grammatical feature of Guyanese Creole, and explain one effect of electronic media on the use of Standard English.}}", "{{3|Firstly, the students rely heavily on abbreviations to communicate quickly.}} Anika writes \"tmrw\" for 'tomorrow', Shonette uses \"b4\" for 'before', and Damion opens with \"ngl\", an acronym for 'not gonna lie'. Ravi even replaces a word with a number that sounds the same, writing \"2\" for 'too'. {{4|These shortened forms save time on a small keyboard, and because every member of the group understands them, they also mark the chat as an informal space shared only by peers.}}", "{{5|Secondly, the students use emoji to supply the non-verbal cues that plain text cannot carry.}} Shonette's \"😩\" conveys her exasperation at having to \"reach home b4 dark\", while Anika's \"😤\" signals mock frustration as she tells the group to \"focus\". Ravi's \"😂😂😂\" stands in for laughter. {{6|In face-to-face conversation, tone of voice and facial expression would convey these emotions; in a chat, emoji perform that function and reduce the risk of a message being misread.}}", "{{7|One grammatical feature of Guyanese Creole in the chat is the use of pre-verbal tense and aspect markers, which are placed before an unchanging verb instead of altering the verb itself.}} {{8|Ravi writes \"me gon walk wid two bottle\", in which \"gon\" marks the future and Standard English requires 'I will bring two bottles'.}} He also writes \"me granny bin mek pholourie\", in which \"bin\" marks the past: 'my grandmother made pholourie'. Anika's \"de minibus does full up Sunday\" uses \"does\" to mark a habitual action: 'the minibus fills up on Sundays'. In each case, the verb remains in its base form.", "{{9|One effect of electronic media on the use of Standard English is that informal digital habits may carry over into formal writing.}} Shonette's \"RAVI U IS DE GOAT\" disregards Standard English spelling and subject-verb agreement, and Anika repeatedly writes \"i\" in lower case, as in \"i mean\". Because students write in this style for hours each day, forms such as \"b4\" and missing punctuation can slip into essays and examination scripts, where they are penalised. {{10|However, this effect is not inevitable: students who understand register can treat chat language as one variety among several and switch to Standard English when the context demands it.}}", "{{11|In conclusion, the chat displays abbreviations and emoji alongside the pre-verbal markers of Guyanese Creole.}} Such habits may weaken students' control of Standard English unless they learn to keep each variety in its proper context."], "notes": ["The context sentence names the medium, participants, place and purpose, which the later analysis of informality depends on.", "The thesis previews the four parts of the question in order, so each later paragraph is expected.", "Point: the first feature is named directly in the topic sentence.", "Explain and Link: the writer explains both the practical reason (speed) and the social function (group identity) of abbreviations.", "The second feature is signposted with 'Secondly' and its purpose is stated at once.", "Explain: emoji are linked to the non-verbal cues of face-to-face talk, showing understanding of communication theory.", "The grammatical feature is named with correct terminology and defined before any example.", "An exact quotation is analysed marker by marker and paired with its Standard English equivalent.", "The effect is stated as a clear cause-and-effect claim in the topic sentence.", "A qualifying counter-point adds balance and sophistication without contradicting the main point.", "The conclusion is shorter than the introduction and summarises without introducing new material."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Content", "of": 10, "got": 10, "why": "Two distinct features of electronic language are named, exemplified and explained; pre-verbal markers are correctly identified with three exact examples and Standard English equivalents; and one effect on Standard English is explained with a balancing qualification."}, {"part": "Organization", "of": 7, "got": 6, "why": "Clear thesis, logical paragraphing and good transitions. One mark held back: the thesis says 'identify' for the first bullet, but the question asks candidates to identify and explain; mirror the question's command words exactly."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 8, "why": "Precise, formal Standard English throughout, with the students' chat language quoted exactly and kept separate from the writer's own analysis."}], "upgrade": {"weak": "Social media is destroying English because teenagers can't spell anymore.", "strong": "Because students write informally for hours each day, forms such as \"b4\" and missing capital letters may slip into formal essays, although students who understand register can switch to Standard English when the context demands it.", "why": "The weak version is an exaggerated opinion with an informal contraction. The strong version explains a cause and an effect, cites the stimulus and adds a balanced qualification in formal Standard English."}}], "m3": {"expository": [{"prompt": "<p>In no more than <b>300 words</b>, write an <b>expository essay</b> explaining three ways in which social media is changing how young Caribbean people communicate.</p>", "title": "How Social Media Is Reshaping Youth Communication in the Caribbean", "essay": ["{{1|On any school bus in Kingstown, most students are communicating, yet few are speaking aloud.}} Platforms such as WhatsApp, TikTok and Instagram have become the main channels through which Caribbean young people share information and maintain relationships. {{2|These platforms are changing youth communication in three main ways: the language young people use, the speed at which information travels and the role of non-verbal cues.}}", "{{3|First, social media is blending Standard English with Creole and new digital forms.}} {{4|A single TikTok caption may combine Vincentian Creole, Jamaican expressions and abbreviations such as \"fr\" and \"ngl\".}} Because these platforms reward short, informal messages, users choose the code that feels most natural and that identifies them with their peers. {{5|As a result,}} many students now switch codes more often and more quickly than earlier generations did.", "Second, social media has increased the speed of communication, and this affects accuracy. When a hurricane approaches, a voice note can reach hundreds of group chats within minutes, often before an official bulletin from the Meteorological Department. However, speed reduces the time users spend checking sources, so rumours travel as quickly as facts. {{6|Consequently, credibility now depends less on the medium and more on the user's ability to verify what is shared.}}", "Finally, emojis, GIFs and voice notes are replacing many face-to-face non-verbal cues. A \"thumbs up\" can substitute for a nod, while a voice note restores the tone of voice that plain text loses. Even so, these substitutes are easily misread across age groups: {{7|what a teenager intends as friendly, a grandparent may interpret as rude.}}", "{{8|In summary, social media is changing the language, speed and non-verbal dimension of youth communication in the Caribbean.}} Understanding these changes helps young people communicate both effectively and responsibly."], "notes": ["A short, concrete Caribbean image hooks the reader without stating an opinion.", "The thesis previews all three points in the same order the body paragraphs follow.", "Point: the topic sentence states one idea only.", "Evidence: a specific, recognisable example rather than a vague claim.", "Explain and Link: the cause-and-effect transition shows why the evidence matters.", "The paragraph ends by linking back to the essay's focus on communication, not on hurricanes.", "Balanced phrasing keeps the tone objective while still giving a vivid illustration.", "The conclusion restates the three points briefly and adds no new argument."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Content", "of": 10, "got": 10, "why": "Three distinct, relevant explanations, each supported with a specific Caribbean example and a clear explanation of effect."}, {"part": "Organization", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "Thesis, three PEEL paragraphs in the promised order, transitions of sequence and consequence, and a brief conclusion."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 7, "why": "Accurate, objective Standard English with varied sentences. One mark held back: the conclusion's final sentence is slightly general."}], "upgrade": {"weak": "I think social media is really bad for how young people talk because they don't use proper English anymore and it's sad.", "strong": "Social media is blending Standard English with Creole and digital abbreviations, because short, informal posts reward the code that identifies users with their peers.", "why": "The weak version gives an opinion and an emotional judgement, which belongs in argument, not exposition. The strong version explains a cause and an effect in neutral, third-person language."}}, {"prompt": "<p>Sargassum seaweed now arrives in large quantities on many Caribbean coastlines. In no more than <b>300 words</b>, write an <b>expository essay</b> explaining the causes and effects of sargassum influxes on Caribbean coastlines and communities.</p>", "title": "The Causes and Effects of Sargassum Influxes in the Caribbean", "essay": ["{{1|On some mornings, the turquoise water at Dennery in Saint Lucia disappears beneath a thick, golden-brown mat.}} This is sargassum, a floating seaweed that has reached Caribbean shores in unusually large quantities since 2011. {{2|These influxes are caused by changing ocean conditions, and they affect both coastal communities and the marine environment.}}", "{{3|The main causes of the influxes are warmer seas and increased nutrients in the Atlantic Ocean.}} Scientists link the growth of large sargassum blooms to rising sea temperatures and to nutrient-rich runoff carried into the ocean by major rivers such as the Amazon. {{4|Because the seaweed feeds on these nutrients, it multiplies rapidly, and ocean currents and trade winds then carry it westward onto the windward coasts of islands such as Barbados and Saint Lucia.}}", "{{5|Consequently, coastal communities face serious health and economic effects.}} As piles of sargassum rot on the shore, they release hydrogen sulphide, a gas that smells like rotten eggs and can irritate the eyes and breathing of nearby residents. {{6|Fishers in Barbados lose income when thick mats tangle their nets and clog boat engines, while hotels must spend heavily on clearing beaches for visitors.}}", "{{7|In addition, sargassum disrupts the marine environment.}} When it piles up on beaches, it can block nesting sea turtles and trap hatchlings trying to reach the sea. Near the shore, decaying seaweed reduces oxygen in the water, harming seagrass beds and the young fish sheltering there. {{8|As a result, an influx that begins as a coastal nuisance can weaken the very ecosystems on which fishing and tourism depend.}}", "{{9|In summary, warmer, nutrient-rich seas cause sargassum influxes, and these influxes affect the health, livelihoods and natural environment of Caribbean coastal communities.}} Understanding both allows communities to respond in an informed way."], "notes": ["A concrete Saint Lucian image hooks the reader and introduces the topic without offering an opinion.", "The thesis previews the structure: causes first, then effects on communities and on the environment.", "Point: the topic sentence names the two causes that the paragraph will explain.", "Explain: the cause-and-effect chain (nutrients, rapid growth, currents and winds) shows how the seaweed reaches specific islands.", "The transition \"Consequently\" links the causes paragraph to the effects, making the logical sequence explicit.", "Evidence: specific, recognisable examples from Barbados show economic effects on two different groups.", "Point: a new topic sentence moves the essay from human effects to environmental effects.", "Link: the paragraph ends by connecting environmental damage back to community livelihoods, tying the essay together.", "The conclusion restates the causes and the three effects in one sentence and adds no new information."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Content", "of": 10, "got": 9, "why": "Accurate causes and a range of well-explained effects, each tied to a named Caribbean territory. One mark held back: the environmental paragraph gives no named location; next step, anchor the turtle example in a specific nesting beach or territory."}, {"part": "Organization", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "The thesis previews a causes-then-effects structure that the body follows exactly, with PEEL paragraphs and clear consequence transitions (Because, Consequently, As a result)."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 7, "why": "Objective, third-person Standard English with precise vocabulary such as \"influx\", \"runoff\" and \"hydrogen sulphide\". One mark held back: the final sentence (\"respond in an informed way\") is vague; next step, end with a sharper restatement of why the topic matters."}], "upgrade": {"weak": "Sargassum is really disgusting and it smells bad and nobody wants to go to the beach anymore because of it.", "strong": "As sargassum rots on the shore, it releases hydrogen sulphide, a gas that smells like rotten eggs and can irritate the eyes and breathing of nearby residents.", "why": "The weak version relies on emotional judgement and exaggeration. The strong version explains the cause of the smell and its effect on people in neutral, precise language, which is what exposition requires."}}, {"prompt": "<p>Carnival is celebrated across the Caribbean region. In no more than <b>300 words</b>, write an <b>expository essay</b> explaining how Carnival communicates Caribbean identity through music, costume and language. Refer to Trinidad and Tobago and at least one other Caribbean territory.</p>", "title": "How Carnival Communicates Caribbean Identity", "essay": ["{{1|Before sunrise on Carnival Monday in Port of Spain, thousands of revellers covered in mud, paint and oil move through the streets to the sound of steelpan and soca.}} This is J'ouvert, the opening of a festival whose modern form was shaped by formerly enslaved Africans after emancipation. {{2|Carnival communicates Caribbean identity through three main channels: its music, its costumes and its language.}}", "{{3|First, Carnival music expresses the region's history and creativity.}} {{4|The steelpan, developed in Trinidad and Tobago during the twentieth century from discarded oil drums, is now the country's national instrument.}} Calypso and soca, the main Carnival genres, use lyrics to comment on social issues, celebrate national pride and invite crowd participation. {{5|Therefore, each road march communicates not only a rhythm but also a shared national story.}}", "{{6|Second, costumes communicate identity visually.}} Traditional characters such as the Moko Jumbie, a stilt-walker linked to West African tradition, preserve African heritage, while large modern mas bands display themes drawn from Caribbean nature and history. In Grenada's Spicemas, Jab Jab masqueraders cover themselves in black oil and wear chains, {{7|a costume that recalls enslavement and the resistance to it.}}", "{{8|Finally, Carnival uses distinctive language that marks participants as members of a community.}} Expressions such as \"playing mas\", \"chipping\" down the road and \"jump and wave\" are understood from Port of Spain to Roseau, and the Midnight Robber's boastful \"robber talk\" displays the power of oral performance. {{9|Because these expressions are learned through participation rather than in classrooms, using them signals belonging to Carnival culture.}}", "{{10|In conclusion, music, costume and language each allow Carnival to communicate a Caribbean identity rooted in history and expressed through creativity.}} From Trinidad and Tobago to Grenada and Sint Maarten, the festival tells the region's story to residents and visitors alike."], "notes": ["A vivid, factual image of J'ouvert hooks the reader while staying objective.", "The thesis names the three channels in the order the body paragraphs will follow.", "Point: the topic sentence states one idea only, music as an expression of history and creativity.", "Evidence: a well-established fact about the steelpan supports the point without invented statistics.", "Link: the consequence transition \"Therefore\" explains what the evidence shows about identity.", "Point: a sequence transition and a clear topic sentence open the second channel.", "Explain: a second territory, Grenada, is used to show the historical meaning carried by a costume.", "Point: the final body paragraph moves to language, completing the order promised in the thesis.", "Explain: the cause-and-effect sentence shows why Carnival vocabulary works as a marker of belonging.", "The conclusion summarises all three channels and introduces no new argument."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Content", "of": 10, "got": 9, "why": "Three relevant channels, each supported by accurate Caribbean examples from Trinidad and Tobago and Grenada. One mark held back: the music paragraph explains Trinidad and Tobago only; next step, add one brief comparison from another territory's Carnival music."}, {"part": "Organization", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "Hook, previewing thesis, three PEEL paragraphs in the promised order and a summary conclusion, linked by sequence and consequence transitions (First, Second, Finally, Therefore, Because)."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 7, "why": "Accurate, neutral Standard English with Carnival terms correctly placed in quotation marks. One mark held back: the closing sentence leans towards a promotional tone (\"tells the region's story\"); next step, keep the final line as neutral as the rest of the essay."}], "upgrade": {"weak": "Carnival is the best festival in the world and it shows how Trinis love to party and have fun.", "strong": "Calypso and soca use lyrics to comment on social issues, celebrate national pride and invite crowd participation, so each road march communicates a shared national story.", "why": "The weak version is an opinion that reduces identity to a stereotype. The strong version explains how a specific feature of Carnival communicates identity, using a clear cause-and-effect link."}}, {"prompt": "<p>Many Caribbean organisations depend on clear communication between staff. In no more than <b>300 words</b>, write an <b>expository essay</b> explaining the main physical, psychological and semantic, and cultural barriers to effective communication in a Caribbean workplace.</p>", "title": "Barriers to Effective Communication in the Caribbean Workplace", "essay": ["{{1|At a busy hotel on the west coast of Barbados, a guest's request for a ground-floor room passes through four staff members before anyone acts on it.}} At each stage, the message can be weakened or distorted. {{2|In Caribbean workplaces such as hotels and banks, three types of barrier commonly prevent effective communication: physical barriers, psychological and semantic barriers, and cultural barriers.}}", "{{3|First, physical barriers interfere with the transmission of a message.}} In the hotel kitchen, the noise of extractor fans and clattering pans can drown out a supervisor's spoken instructions, while staff on different shifts may rarely meet face to face. {{4|As a result, instructions are often passed on second-hand through notes or text messages, which increases the chance that important details are lost.}}", "{{5|Second, psychological and semantic barriers affect how a message is received and understood.}} A new teller at a bank in Georgetown, Guyana, who feels anxious in front of a senior manager may simply nod instead of asking questions. Similarly, banking jargon such as \"KYC compliance\" or \"overdraft facility\" may mean little to a new employee or customer. {{6|Consequently, the receiver may appear to understand a message that has not truly been understood.}}", "{{7|Finally, cultural barriers arise because Caribbean workforces are diverse.}} Guyana's population includes people of Indian, African, Indigenous, Chinese, Portuguese and mixed heritage, while hotels in Barbados often employ staff from several territories and countries. Expectations about directness, eye contact and how to address senior colleagues may differ between these groups. {{8|Therefore, feedback that a manager intends as efficient may be received as disrespectful, which damages working relationships.}}", "{{9|In summary, physical, psychological and semantic, and cultural barriers can each distort messages in Caribbean workplaces.}} Recognising these barriers is the first step towards clear communication at work."], "notes": ["A specific Barbados workplace scenario hooks the reader and illustrates how a message can be distorted.", "The thesis names all three barrier types in the exact order the body paragraphs follow.", "Point: the topic sentence defines the barrier in terms of the communication process (transmission).", "Explain and Link: the consequence transition shows how a physical barrier leads to lost information.", "Point: the topic sentence groups psychological and semantic barriers as both affecting reception.", "Link: the paragraph ends with a clear effect, false understanding, rather than simply listing examples.", "Point: the topic sentence states the cause of cultural barriers in one clear idea.", "Explain: a balanced cause-and-effect sentence shows a misunderstanding without blaming either group.", "The conclusion restates the three barriers concisely and adds no new information."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Content", "of": 10, "got": 10, "why": "All three barrier types are explained accurately with realistic examples from a Barbados hotel and a Guyana bank, and each explanation shows its effect on the message."}, {"part": "Organization", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "Hook, previewing thesis, three PEEL paragraphs in the promised order and a brief summary conclusion, connected by sequence and consequence transitions."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 7, "why": "Precise, objective Standard English with accurate communication terminology. One mark held back: the repeated phrase \"physical, psychological and semantic, and cultural\" is clumsy; next step, vary it in the conclusion (for example, \"environmental, personal and cultural barriers\")."}], "upgrade": {"weak": "Workers in the Caribbean can't communicate properly because they are always too loud and don't listen to their bosses.", "strong": "In the hotel kitchen, the noise of extractor fans and clattering pans can drown out a supervisor's spoken instructions, so details are often lost.", "why": "The weak version is a sweeping generalisation that blames people. The strong version identifies a specific physical barrier and explains its effect on the message objectively."}}], "argumentative": [{"prompt": "<p>\"Caribbean Creole languages should be used alongside Standard English as languages of instruction in primary schools.\"</p><p>In no more than <b>300 words</b>, write an <b>argumentative essay</b> in which you take a position on this statement.</p>", "title": "Teaching in the Language Children Bring", "essay": ["Many Caribbean children begin school speaking a Creole at home, yet are taught entirely in Standard English from their first day. {{1|Although Standard English remains essential for regional examinations and international communication,}} {{2|Creole languages should be used alongside it as languages of instruction in primary schools, because doing so improves understanding, strengthens literacy in both languages and affirms children's identity.}}", "{{3|Children learn best in a language they already understand.}} A seven-year-old in Antigua who is asked a question in Antiguan Creole can show what she knows; asked the same question in formal Standard English, she may remain silent. When teachers explain new concepts in the home language first, learners grasp the idea before facing an unfamiliar code. Understanding, not vocabulary, therefore becomes the measure of learning.", "{{4|Moreover, using Creole can strengthen, rather than weaken, Standard English.}} {{5|The Bilingual Education Project piloted in Jamaican primary schools suggested that pupils who first read and write in their home language transfer those skills to the official language more successfully.}} Comparing the two systems also shows children that each language has its own rules, which makes Standard English easier to master.", "{{6|Critics argue that Creole has no place in the classroom because parents want their children to succeed in examinations set in Standard English.}} {{7|This concern is understandable; however, the proposal is not to replace Standard English but to build a bridge to it.}} Children who feel that their language is valued participate more confidently, and confident learners achieve more.", "{{8|Using Creole alongside Standard English is therefore not a step backward but a practical strategy for better learning.}} Caribbean primary schools should teach in the language children bring while guiding them towards the language they will need."], "notes": ["A concession in the thesis shows the writer has weighed both sides before choosing one.", "A complete position plus three reasons: the reader knows exactly what will be argued.", "Point: a logical claim that the paragraph then proves with an example.", "Transition of addition introduces the second reason.", "Evidence: a named regional initiative gives the argument credibility (logos and ethos).", "The counter-argument is stated fairly, in its strongest form.", "Rebuttal: \"however\" turns the objection into support for the writer's position.", "The conclusion restates the thesis in fresh words and ends with a balanced, memorable line."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Content", "of": 10, "got": 9, "why": "A clear position, two well-supported reasons and a fair, rebutted counter-argument. One mark held back: the third reason (identity) is folded into the rebuttal rather than developed fully."}, {"part": "Organization", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "Logical sequence from thesis to reasons to counter-argument and conclusion, linked by precise transitions."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 8, "why": "Formal, measured and accurate Standard English; balanced sentences (\"not to replace... but to build\") give the argument authority."}], "upgrade": {"weak": "Some people say Creole is bad for school but they are wrong and don't know what they are talking about.", "strong": "Critics argue that Creole has no place in the classroom because parents want examination success; however, the proposal is to build a bridge to Standard English, not to replace it.", "why": "The weak version dismisses the other side. The strong version states the objection fairly, then answers it with reasoning, which is what earns Content marks in an argument."}}, {"prompt": "<p>\"Cellphones should be banned in Caribbean secondary schools.\"</p><p>In no more than <b>300 words</b>, write an <b>argumentative essay</b> in which you take a position on this statement.</p>", "title": "Regulate the Phone, Do Not Ban It", "essay": ["{{1|Few objects cause more arguments in a Caribbean staffroom than the cellphone.}} Teachers in Philipsburg, Bridgetown and San Fernando rightly complain that phones distract students during lessons. {{2|However, a complete ban would be the wrong response: cellphones should be regulated, not banned, because clear rules reduce distraction, phones support learning and they keep students safe.}}", "{{3|First, regulation addresses the real problem, which is misuse rather than the device itself.}} A school that requires phones to be switched off and stored during lessons, and allows them only at lunch or when a teacher directs, removes most distraction. A ban, by contrast, often drives phones into pockets and bathrooms, where they are harder to supervise. {{4|It is therefore more logical to control when phones are used than to pretend they do not exist.}}", "{{5|Moreover, phones are useful learning tools.}} {{6|A Fifth Form student in Belize can photograph notes, check a definition, record a Spanish oral practice or submit work on Google Classroom from the same device.}} In schools where computer labs are limited, the phone is often the only technology every student carries.", "{{7|Critics argue that phones encourage cyberbullying and damage concentration, and these dangers are real.}} {{8|Nevertheless, a ban does not end cyberbullying, which happens mostly outside school hours; teaching responsible use does.}} Furthermore, during hurricane season or a sudden flash flood, parents need to reach their children quickly, and a phone in the school bag can provide that reassurance.", "{{9|Banning cellphones would solve a visible problem while creating several hidden ones.}} {{10|Caribbean secondary schools should instead set firm, fair rules that keep phones silent in lessons but available for learning and safety.}}"], "notes": ["A familiar, specific opening hook draws the reader in without emotional exaggeration.", "The thesis states a complete position (regulate, not ban) and previews three reasons, guided by a contrast transition.", "Point: the first reason is stated as a clear logical claim that redefines the problem.", "Link: \"therefore\" draws a logical conclusion and ties the paragraph back to the thesis.", "A transition of addition introduces the second reason.", "Evidence: concrete, local examples make the claim about learning credible and checkable (logos).", "The counter-argument is stated fairly and even conceded in part, which strengthens the writer's ethos.", "Rebuttal: \"Nevertheless\" turns the objection around by showing that a ban does not solve the problem raised.", "The conclusion restates the position in fresh words through a balanced contrast of visible and hidden problems.", "The final sentence gives a practical recommendation that echoes all three reasons without repeating the thesis word for word."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Content", "of": 10, "got": 9, "why": "A clear, complete position, three relevant reasons and a fair counter-argument that is properly rebutted. One mark held back: the safety reason is added at the end of the rebuttal paragraph rather than developed in its own PEEL paragraph; next step, give it a separate point with its own evidence."}, {"part": "Organization", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "Each body paragraph follows PEEL, and transitions of addition and contrast (First, Moreover, Nevertheless, Furthermore) move the argument logically to a conclusion that mirrors the thesis."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 8, "why": "Formal, measured and accurate Standard English; balanced structures such as \"regulated, not banned\" and \"a visible problem... hidden ones\" give the argument authority."}], "upgrade": {"weak": "Banning phones is a stupid idea because everybody needs their phone and teachers just want to control us.", "strong": "Critics argue that phones encourage cyberbullying and damage concentration; nevertheless, a ban does not end cyberbullying, while teaching responsible use does.", "why": "The weak version insults the other side and relies on feeling. The strong version takes the opposing concern seriously and answers it with reasoning, which is what an argumentative essay is rewarded for."}}, {"prompt": "<p>\"Tourism does more harm than good to small Caribbean islands.\"</p><p>In no more than <b>300 words</b>, write an <b>argumentative essay</b> in which you take a position on this statement.</p>", "title": "Tourism Managed at Home", "essay": ["Every morning, cruise ships line the harbours of Castries, St. John's and Philipsburg, and every evening they leave behind both income and litter. {{1|Tourism undeniably places pressure on the environment and sends part of its earnings overseas.}} {{2|However, it does more good than harm to small Caribbean islands when it is locally managed, because it creates jobs, supports local producers and funds conservation.}}", "{{3|First, tourism is a major employer on islands with few alternative industries.}} Hotels, tour operators, taxi drivers and craft vendors all depend on visitors. {{4|A young Grenadian who trains in hospitality at a community college can find work at home rather than migrating, which keeps skills and families on the island.}}", "{{5|Moreover, local management keeps more of the money in the community.}} {{6|Critics rightly point out that much tourist spending \"leaks\" away when resorts are foreign-owned and import their food.}} {{7|Yet this leakage is a problem of management, not of tourism itself.}} When hotels buy fish from local fishers and fruit from Saint Lucian farmers, and when visitors stay in family-run guesthouses in Dominica, earnings remain in the island economy.", "{{8|Similarly, the environmental harm can be reduced by the very income tourism produces.}} Entrance fees to marine parks and nature trails help pay for reef protection and rangers. Because visitors come to see clean beaches and healthy coral, islands have a strong financial reason to protect them.", "{{9|The harms of tourism are real, but they are signs of poor planning rather than proof that the industry should be rejected.}} {{10|When Caribbean people own, supply and regulate it, tourism remains one of the region's most valuable resources.}}"], "notes": ["A concession acknowledges real harms at the outset, showing the writer has weighed the evidence fairly.", "The thesis states a complete, qualified position (\"when it is locally managed\") and previews three reasons.", "Point: a clear logical claim based on a well-established feature of small island economies.", "Evidence: a specific, realistic Caribbean example illustrates the claim and links it to the problem of migration.", "A transition of addition introduces the second reason.", "The counter-argument is stated fairly, using the accurate economic term \"leakage\".", "Rebuttal: \"Yet\" reframes the objection as a management problem, which supports the writer's qualified position.", "\"Similarly\" links the third reason and answers the environmental objection with logic rather than denial.", "The conclusion restates the position in fresh words, conceding the harms while explaining their cause.", "The final sentence names who should manage tourism, giving the argument a clear and memorable resolution."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Content", "of": 10, "got": 9, "why": "A clear, qualified position, three relevant reasons and a fair treatment of both harms named in the question. One mark held back: the conservation paragraph asserts that fees fund protection without a concrete example; next step, add one specific illustration, such as a named marine park, to support the point."}, {"part": "Organization", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "PEEL paragraphs flow logically, the counter-argument is placed where it is most relevant, and transitions of addition and contrast (First, Moreover, Yet, Similarly) guide the reader to a conclusion that mirrors the thesis."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 7, "why": "Formal, precise and accurate, with effective balanced sentences. One mark held back: the opening sentence leans towards description; next step, sharpen it so that the hook leads more directly into the argument."}], "upgrade": {"weak": "Tourism is good for the Caribbean and it brings a lot of money, so people should stop complaining about it.", "strong": "Critics rightly point out that much tourist spending leaks away when resorts are foreign-owned; yet this leakage is a problem of management, not of tourism itself.", "why": "The weak version ignores the real harms and dismisses opponents. The strong version accepts the evidence against tourism and then shows, through reasoning, why it does not defeat the writer's position."}}, {"prompt": "<p>\"Social media influencers are now more trustworthy sources of news than traditional media.\"</p><p>In no more than <b>300 words</b>, write an <b>argumentative essay</b> in which you take a position on this statement.</p>", "title": "Popularity Is Not Credibility", "essay": ["When a storm forms in the Atlantic, many young people in Kingston or Georgetown check TikTok before they check a newspaper or a radio station. {{1|Influencers are certainly faster and more familiar to young audiences.}} {{2|However, they are not more trustworthy than traditional media, because a credible source needs expertise, accountability and verification, and on these measures traditional media remain stronger.}}", "{{3|First, credibility depends on expertise.}} A national meteorological service employs trained forecasters, and broadcasters report its official bulletins. {{4|An influencer with thousands of followers in Belize may be entertaining, but popularity is not proof of knowledge.}}", "{{5|Moreover, traditional media are accountable for what they publish.}} {{6|A newspaper such as the Jamaica Gleaner can be required to print a correction, and its journalists work under editors and press codes.}} An influencer who spreads a false rumour about a hurricane's path can simply delete the post, leaving the damage behind.", "{{7|Critics argue that traditional media are slow and that influencers offer currency, reporting events as they happen.}} {{8|This is true; nevertheless, speed without verification is a weakness, not a strength.}} Professional newsrooms confirm facts with more than one source before broadcasting, while viral voice notes on WhatsApp often circulate unchecked. Information that is current but false can cause panic, as when residents rush to buy supplies because of a warning.", "{{9|Influencers have earned their audiences through speed and personality, but trust must be earned through expertise, accountability and careful checking.}} {{10|Until influencers meet those standards, traditional media remain the more reliable source of Caribbean news.}}"], "notes": ["A concession grants the influencers' genuine advantage, showing the writer has considered both sides.", "The thesis takes a complete position and names the credibility criteria that will structure the argument.", "Point: the first criterion, expertise, is stated directly as the paragraph's claim.", "Explain: a balanced contrast exposes the logical flaw of treating popularity as proof of knowledge.", "A transition of addition introduces the second criterion, accountability.", "Evidence: a real regional newspaper, referred to in general terms, illustrates how editorial accountability works.", "The counter-argument is stated fairly in its strongest form, using the credibility criterion of currency.", "Rebuttal: the writer concedes the point, then uses \"nevertheless\" to show that speed without verification weakens credibility.", "The conclusion restates the position in fresh words by contrasting how audiences and trust are earned.", "The final sentence leaves room for change (\"Until\") while clearly reaffirming the writer's position."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Content", "of": 10, "got": 10, "why": "A clear, complete position defended through all four credibility criteria named in the course: expertise, accountability and verification as reasons, and currency handled as the counter-argument. Evidence is realistic and regional, and the rebuttal is logical rather than dismissive."}, {"part": "Organization", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "The thesis previews the criteria, each body paragraph develops one of them using PEEL, and transitions of addition and contrast (First, Moreover, nevertheless) lead to a conclusion that mirrors the opening."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 7, "why": "Formal, measured and accurate Standard English with effective balanced sentences. One mark held back: \"First, credibility depends on expertise\" is slightly abrupt; next step, vary the topic sentences so each one also hints at the contrast being drawn."}], "upgrade": {"weak": "Influencers are just people with phones who lie to get likes, so nobody should believe anything they say.", "strong": "Critics argue that influencers offer currency by reporting events as they happen. This is true; nevertheless, speed without verification is a weakness, not a strength.", "why": "The weak version overgeneralises and attacks people rather than ideas. The strong version measures influencers against a recognised credibility criterion, concedes what is valid and then rebuts with logic."}}], "persuasive": [{"prompt": "<p>You are president of your school's Environmental Club. In no more than <b>300 words</b>, write a <b>persuasive speech</b> to deliver at morning assembly, urging students to support a new recycling drive.</p>", "title": "The Smoke Is Our Message", "essay": ["Good morning, fellow students. {{1|Last month, many of us woke to the smell of smoke drifting from the landfill on Pond Island. Some of us coughed through first period; some of our younger brothers and sisters stayed home altogether.}} {{2|That smoke carries a message, and this morning I am asking you to answer it: support our recycling drive and cut the waste we send to that landfill.}}", "{{3|As your Environmental Club president, I have spent the last three weeks sorting the bins behind the cafeteria.}} Most of what we throw away could have been reused or recycled. {{4|Think about what passes through your hands in a single school day: a plastic bottle, a styrofoam lunch box, a juice pouch.}} Multiply that by every student in this hall, five days a week. {{5|Our side of the island measures only about 34 square kilometres. There is no \"away\" to throw things to.}}", "{{6|Some of you are thinking, \"What difference can one bottle make?\"}} One bottle is nothing. One school is something. {{7|One school that refuses to feed the fire is a lesson the whole island can learn.}}", "{{8|So here is what I am asking. Starting Monday, there will be blue bins on every corridor. Bring a refillable bottle. Sort before you throw. Challenge your form class to fill more blue bins than any other.}} {{9|This hurricane season reminded us how fragile our island is; let us not wait for the next fire to remind us again.}}", "{{10|The smoke sent us a message. Let our answer be clear: not on our watch.}} Thank you."], "notes": ["Pathos: a shared, local experience makes the problem personal for this audience.", "The thesis and the action requested are stated early, so the audience knows the purpose.", "Ethos: the speaker's role and first-hand work establish credibility.", "Direct address and a triad make the problem concrete and memorable.", "Logos: a verifiable fact about the island's size supports the argument.", "A rhetorical question voices the audience's likely objection.", "Repetition with escalation (\"One bottle... One school... One school that...\") answers the objection.", "A specific, achievable call to action in short imperative sentences.", "Kairos: the recent hurricane season makes acting now feel urgent.", "The closing line echoes the opening image, giving the speech a strong sense of completion."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Content", "of": 10, "got": 9, "why": "All four appeals are used deliberately for a clearly defined audience, the objection is answered and the call to action is specific. One mark held back: the logos would be stronger with one figure from the club's own bin audit."}, {"part": "Organization", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "Hook, purpose, credibility and evidence, objection, call to action and an echoing close: a complete persuasive arc."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 8, "why": "Register suits an assembly speech; varied sentence length, triads and repetition are controlled and purposeful."}], "upgrade": {"weak": "Recycling is very important and everyone should do it because it is good for the environment.", "strong": "Bring a refillable bottle. Sort before you throw. Challenge your form class to fill more blue bins than any other.", "why": "The weak version is a general statement that asks for nothing specific. The strong version gives the audience three clear, achievable actions in short, direct sentences."}}, {"prompt": "<p>Residents of Barbados have reported hatchlings wandering towards beachfront lights and litter piling up on nesting beaches. You are a resident who volunteers on night turtle patrols. In no more than <b>300 words</b>, write a <b>persuasive</b> letter to the editor of a national newspaper urging residents to protect the island's turtle nesting beaches.</p>", "title": "Give Our Turtles the Dark", "essay": ["Dear Editor,", "{{1|Just after midnight last Saturday, on the sand near Mullins in St. Peter, I watched a line of hawksbill hatchlings crawl away from the sea, towards the glare of a beachfront bar.}} We carried as many as we could to the water. {{2|I am writing to urge every resident to help keep our nesting beaches dark, clean and undisturbed.}}", "{{3|For six years I have volunteered on west coast night patrols, marking nests and guiding hatchlings.}} {{4|Hatchlings find the ocean by crawling towards the brightest horizon, which on a natural beach is moonlight on the water. One floodlight can pull them inland, where exhaustion, dogs or traffic finish them.}} Litter is no kinder: a drifting plastic bag looks like a jellyfish to a hungry turtle.", "{{5|Some will say that turtles are a matter for conservationists and tourism officials, not ordinary Barbadians.}} Yet those hatchlings did not crawl towards that bar because of any official; someone simply left a light on. {{6|The problem begins in our yards, our cars and our beach bags, and so must the solution.}}", "{{7|So I ask your readers to do three things. Switch off or shield any light that shines onto the sand after dark. Take home every bottle, bag and straw you carry to the beach. Keep clear of marked nests and report any disturbed nest.}}", "{{8|Hatching season is still under way, and a female that survives tonight may return to the same beach to nest as an adult.}} {{9|What will she find: a shore that welcomes her, or one that lures her young away from the sea?}}", "{{10|Let us give our turtles the one thing they need from us, the dark, so that the next line of hatchlings crawls towards the moon, not the bar.}}", "Yours faithfully,<br>Andrea Griffith<br>St. James"], "notes": ["Pathos: a vivid, first-hand scene at a named Barbadian beach draws the reader in and makes the problem immediate.", "The purpose of the letter is stated early, with a triad (dark, clean and undisturbed) that previews the three threats.", "Ethos: six years of volunteer patrols establish the writer as someone who knows the beaches first-hand.", "Logos: a well-established fact about how hatchlings navigate explains exactly why artificial light is deadly.", "The likely objection, that this is someone else's job, is anticipated fairly before being answered.", "A triad of everyday places returns responsibility to ordinary residents and answers the objection memorably.", "A specific, achievable call to action in short imperative sentences that match the three threats named earlier.", "Kairos: the season still being under way, and the long life of a returning female, make acting now feel urgent.", "A rhetorical question presents a stark choice and leaves readers to supply the answer themselves.", "The closing line echoes the opening image of the moon and the bar's glare, giving the letter a strong sense of completion."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Content", "of": 10, "got": 9, "why": "Ethos, pathos, logos and kairos are all deliberate, the objection is fairly answered and the call to action is specific and realistic. One mark held back: the logos rests on a single fact; one observation from the writer's own patrol records (for example, how many nests were disturbed this season) would strengthen it."}, {"part": "Organization", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "Correct letter conventions (salutation, formal sign-off, name and parish) frame a complete persuasive arc: hook, purpose, credibility and evidence, objection, call to action and an echoing close."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 8, "why": "The register suits a public letter: formal but warm. Triads, imagery and a rhetorical question are controlled, and sentence length varies with purpose."}], "upgrade": {"weak": "People should stop putting lights near the beach because it is bad for turtles.", "strong": "Hatchlings find the ocean by crawling towards the brightest horizon, which on a natural beach is moonlight on the water. One floodlight can pull them inland.", "why": "The weak version asserts a conclusion without a reason. The strong version explains the cause, so readers understand why their own light matters and are more likely to act."}}, {"prompt": "<p>A youth radio station in Trinidad and Tobago is running a campaign to encourage young people to give blood. In no more than <b>300 words</b>, write a <b>persuasive</b> script for a one-minute radio public service announcement urging listeners aged 17 and over to become first-time blood donors at the national blood service. You may use speaker labels and sound-effect directions.</p>", "title": "Be the Reason the Wait Ends", "essay": ["[SFX: A hospital monitor beeps steadily. A soft steelpan melody rises underneath.]", "ANNOUNCER: {{1|Somewhere in Trinidad and Tobago right now, a patient is waiting. Maybe it is a mother after a difficult birth. Maybe it is a child living with sickle cell. Maybe it is your best friend after a crash on the highway.}}", "ANNOUNCER: {{2|If you are seventeen or older, you can be the reason that wait ends. Become a first-time blood donor at the National Blood Bank.}}", "NADIA (19, from Chaguanas): {{3|I was nervous my first time, not going to lie. But the needle was just a pinch, the nurses were kind, and I left with juice, a biscuit and the best feeling all week.}}", "ANNOUNCER: {{4|Blood cannot be made in a factory or bought off a shelf. It comes from only one place: people like you.}} {{5|And because one donation can be separated into different parts, a single visit can help more than one patient.}}", "ANNOUNCER: {{6|Thinking someone else will do it? If everyone thinks someone else will give, then no one gives.}} {{7|You lime together, you fete together, you study together. Now give together.}}", "ANNOUNCER: {{8|Here is how. Eat a proper meal, drink plenty of water, bring your ID (and, at seventeen, a parental consent form) and walk in to the National Blood Bank or a donation drive near you. Bring a friend. Make it your first time, not your last.}}", "ANNOUNCER: {{9|Christmas is coming. This year, give a gift that no shop on Frederick Street can sell.}}", "[SFX: The monitor beeps once more. Music swells.]", "ANNOUNCER: {{10|Somewhere, a patient is still waiting. Seventeen and over? Be the reason the wait ends.}}"], "notes": ["Pathos and repetition (anaphora on \"Maybe it is\") build a triad of patients, ending with the most personal one for a young listener.", "The audience and the action are named immediately, which a radio listener needs because they cannot reread.", "Ethos and reassurance: a peer's voice in relaxed, conversational English is more credible to teenagers than an official one.", "Logos: a plain, uncontroversial fact explains why donors are essential, using a simple contrast that is easy to hear.", "Logos: a well-established fact about separating blood components shows the value of one visit without inventing figures.", "A rhetorical question voices the listener's likely excuse, and a balanced, repetitive answer dismantles it.", "A rhythmic triad of familiar youth activities, capped by a fourth, turns giving blood into a shared, social act.", "A specific, achievable call to action in short imperatives the listener can remember and follow.", "Kairos: the approaching Christmas season and a local reference (Frederick Street) make the appeal timely and familiar.", "The close repeats the opening image and the key slogan, so the message stays in the listener's ear after the music ends."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Content", "of": 10, "got": 9, "why": "The audience is clear, all four appeals are used and the objection is answered crisply; the call to action is practical and avoids invented statistics. One mark held back: the script does not tell listeners where to find opening hours or drive locations; a line directing them to the service's official channels would complete the action."}, {"part": "Organization", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "Sound cues, speaker labels and short segments suit a one-minute broadcast, moving from hook to purpose, testimony, reasons, objection, call to action and an echoing close."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 7, "why": "Short, spoken sentences, repetition and a memorable slogan suit radio. One mark held back: \"You lime together, you fete together\" is lively but sits slightly uneasily beside a serious medical message; a tighter link back to the patient would keep the tone consistent."}], "upgrade": {"weak": "ANNOUNCER: Giving blood is a good thing to do and you should think about donating if you can.", "strong": "ANNOUNCER: If you are seventeen or older, you can be the reason that wait ends. Become a first-time blood donor at the National Blood Bank.", "why": "The weak line is vague and tentative (\"think about\", \"if you can\"). The strong line names the audience, the action and the place, and links them to the waiting patient in a slogan listeners can remember."}}, {"prompt": "<p>A regional youth magazine is publishing a summer safety issue. In no more than <b>300 words</b>, write a <b>persuasive</b> feature article persuading Caribbean teenagers to learn to swim and to learn water safety. Refer to beaches and rivers, and include examples from Guyana and Jamaica.</p>", "title": "Surrounded by Water: Why This Is Your Year to Swim", "essay": ["{{1|We live surrounded by water, yet many of us could not save ourselves in it.}} From the beaches of Negril to the dark creeks of Guyana's interior, water shapes Caribbean life: our limes, our holidays, our weekends. {{2|If you cannot swim yet, this is your year to learn, and to learn water safety too.}}", "{{3|Health organisations count drowning among the leading causes of death for children and young people worldwide, and most drownings can be prevented.}} {{4|Picture it: a Sunday river lime on the Essequibo, music playing, curry in the pot, and a friend who steps off a sandbank into a current nobody saw.}} Guyana's rivers can look calm on top while fast water moves beneath, especially after rain. On Jamaica's north coast, a rip current can drag even a confident swimmer from shore in seconds.", "{{5|Ask Devon Thompson, 17, from Ocho Rios. \"Mi did think mi could swim,\" he says. \"Then the current tek mi.\"}} A lifeguard reached him in time. {{6|He has since completed a course and knows to escape a rip by swimming parallel to the shore, not against it.}}", "{{7|Maybe you are thinking, \"I'm too old to start, and it's embarrassing.\"}} It is far more embarrassing to stand frozen on the sand while a friend shouts for help. Many coaches teach teenagers in small, patient groups, and progress comes quickly.", "{{8|So take the plunge, safely. Ask your school, a swimming club or a community centre about lessons. Learn to float, to tread water and to spot a rip. Never swim alone, and never swim in a river after heavy rain.}} {{9|Summer brings beach days and river trips; start now, before the next invitation arrives.}}", "{{10|We are surrounded by water. Let us make sure it surrounds us with joy, not grief.}}"], "notes": ["A paradoxical opening hook speaks directly to island readers and frames the whole article.", "Direct address states the purpose plainly, so the reader knows exactly what they are being persuaded to do.", "Ethos and logos: a well-established global fact, attributed in general terms, gives the argument authority without inventing figures.", "Pathos: vivid sensory imagery of a familiar Guyanese river lime turns an abstract danger into a scene the reader can see.", "A short testimony from a peer, with Jamaican Creole kept inside the dialogue, makes the danger real and relatable.", "Logos: a practical safety rule shows that skills, not luck, save lives, and gives the story a hopeful turn.", "The reader's likely objection is voiced in their own words, then answered with a sharp contrast.", "A specific, achievable call to action in short imperatives, including a triad of skills to learn.", "Kairos: the summer season of beach and river trips gives readers a reason to act now.", "The close repeats the opening phrase and ends on an antithesis (joy, not grief), making the message memorable."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Content", "of": 10, "got": 10, "why": "Persuasive appeals are balanced and well chosen for teenagers; Guyanese and Jamaican examples are specific and accurate; the objection is answered and the call to action gives concrete steps and safety rules."}, {"part": "Organization", "of": 7, "got": 6, "why": "The arc from hook to purpose, evidence, story, objection and call to action is clear. One mark held back: a feature article usually carries a short standfirst or subheadings; adding a one-line standfirst under the title would better suit the magazine form."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 8, "why": "A lively, conversational register suits a youth magazine; imagery, triads and antithesis are purposeful, and Creole is correctly confined to dialogue."}], "upgrade": {"weak": "Swimming is a very important skill and teenagers should learn it because the Caribbean has a lot of beaches.", "strong": "Picture it: a Sunday river lime on the Essequibo, music playing, curry in the pot, and a friend who steps off a sandbank into a current nobody saw.", "why": "The weak version states an obvious point with no emotional pull. The strong version places the reader inside a familiar scene, so the danger is felt rather than simply told."}}], "narrative": [{"prompt": "<p>In no more than <b>300 words</b>, write a <b>narrative</b> about a time when a misunderstood message taught someone the importance of careful listening.</p>", "title": "The Big Pot", "essay": ["{{1|The message from Granny Lena arrived at 4:12 on a Saturday afternoon: \"Come by me now. Bring the big pot. Don't tell nobody.\"}}", "{{2|I read it once and grinned.}} Granny's goat water was famous from Brades to Salem, and \"the big pot\" could only mean one thing. Ignoring the last line, I texted my cousins, Malik and Tasha. Within twenty minutes we were squeezed into Malik's car, the dented pot rattling on the back seat.", "{{3|The sky over the hills had turned the colour of an old bruise by the time we pulled into her yard. No smoke rose from the kitchen. No smell of thyme and scotch bonnet greeted us. Instead, we heard a steady drip, drip, drip.}}", "{{4|Granny stood in the middle of her living room, a dish towel over her shoulder, staring at the ceiling.}} A brown stain had spread above her favourite armchair, and water was falling onto the cushion.", "{{5|\"I said don't tell nobody,\" she sighed. \"I didn't want the whole family worrying about an old woman's roof.\"}}", "{{6|My face grew hot.}} I set the pot under the leak, and its hollow ping filled the silence. Malik climbed onto a chair to check the ceiling while Tasha lifted the photographs off the shelf. Nobody mentioned goat water.", "That evening, as the rain eased, Granny finally laughed. \"At least I had three helpers instead of one.\"", "{{7|I laughed too, but I had learned something. I had read her words, yet I had heard only what I wanted to hear.}} {{8|Since that afternoon, I read every message twice: once for what I expect, and once for what it really says.}}"], "notes": ["Orientation and hook: the message itself opens the story and plants the key detail (\"Don't tell nobody\").", "Short sentence, first person, past tense: the narrator's mistake is set up quickly.", "Complication shown through sensory detail and contrast (what is missing, what is heard) with short sentences that build tension.", "Climax: the reader sees the truth through an image, not an explanation.", "Dialogue in Montserrat Creole reveals character; the narration around it remains in Standard English.", "Show, don't tell: embarrassment is revealed through a physical reaction.", "Reflection links the events to the theme of listening.", "The ending echoes the opening (\"read it once\" becomes \"read every message twice\"), giving the story a satisfying circle."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Content", "of": 10, "got": 10, "why": "An engaging, original story that fully addresses the task: the misunderstanding, its consequence and a clear insight about listening."}, {"part": "Organization", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "Complete narrative arc with controlled pacing; the opening and closing mirror each other."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 7, "why": "Vivid, precise imagery and accurate Standard English in the narration. One mark held back: the reflection could be tightened into one sharper sentence."}], "upgrade": {"weak": "When I got there I was very surprised and embarrassed because the roof was leaking and there was no food.", "strong": "No smoke rose from the kitchen. No smell of thyme and scotch bonnet greeted us. Instead, we heard a steady drip, drip, drip.", "why": "The weak version tells the reader the feeling. The strong version shows the scene through sound and smell, so the reader discovers the truth with the narrator."}}, {"prompt": "<p>In no more than <b>300 words</b>, write a <b>narrative</b> about a student who moves to a new Caribbean island and discovers that language can both separate and connect people.</p>", "title": "The Real Way", "essay": ["{{1|On my first morning in Sint Maarten, I discovered that I spoke English and that nobody understood me.}}", "Mummy had found work at a hotel in Simpson Bay, so I left Berbice with one suitcase, a jar of mango achar and an accent I had never noticed. In my new Form Four class, the teacher asked me to introduce myself. {{2|\"Me name Anjali, and me does live by Cole Bay now,\" I said.}}", "{{3|Somebody snorted. Somebody else repeated \"me does\" in a squeaky voice. The laughter rolled across the room like a wave over sand, and I studied the scratches on my desk until the bell rang.}}", "For two weeks I barely spoke. I flattened my vowels and ate my roti alone beneath the almond tree. {{4|Silence, I decided, was safer than sound.}}", "One lunchtime, a tall boy named Kemar dropped onto the bench beside me. He leaned towards my lunch box and sniffed.", "{{5|\"Wha' dat is? It smelling sweet, yes.\"}}", "\"Roti and curry chicken,\" I said carefully.", "{{6|\"My granny come from Guyana,\" he said, grinning. \"She does talk just like you. Say something else, nah. Say it the real way.\"}}", "{{7|I hesitated. Then the words came tumbling out, rich and unfiltered, and Kemar laughed. This time, though, he was laughing with me, not at me.}} By Friday, half the class was greeting me with a clumsy \"Wha' happen, gyal?\" and I was answering in my best Sint Maarten lilt.", "{{8|I learned that language can build a wall in a single sentence and open a gate in the next. The difference is whether the listener wants to keep me out or let me in.}}", "{{9|I still speak an English all my own. Now, though, everybody understands me, even when they miss a word.}}"], "notes": ["Orientation and hook: a paradox (speaking English yet not being understood) introduces the theme of language at once.", "Dialogue in Guyanese Creole reveals the narrator's identity, while the surrounding narration stays in Standard English.", "Complication shown through sound and a simile, with short sentences that let the embarrassment land without being named.", "A short, aphoristic sentence slows the pacing and captures the narrator's withdrawal.", "Sint Maarten English in dialogue marks the turning point and shows Kemar's curiosity rather than mockery.", "Climax: a shared family link reveals that the very language that isolated the narrator can also connect her.", "Resolution shown through action and a precise contrast between laughing \"at\" and \"with\".", "Reflection states the insight about language and the listener's attitude without lecturing the reader.", "The ending echoes the opening line, turning \"nobody understood me\" into \"everybody understands me\" for a satisfying circle."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Content", "of": 10, "got": 10, "why": "Fully addresses the prompt: language first separates the narrator (mockery of her Creole) and then connects her (a shared Guyanese link), with a clear insight about listening and belonging."}, {"part": "Organization", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "Complete arc from orientation to reflection; pacing moves from fast embarrassment to slow withdrawal to a quick turning point, and the final line mirrors the first."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 7, "why": "Precise imagery and accurate Standard English narration, with Creole confined to dialogue. One mark held back: the simile \"like a wave over sand\" is familiar; a fresher, more personal image would lift the complication further."}], "upgrade": {"weak": "Everybody laughed at my accent and I felt very embarrassed and sad, so I did not talk much after that.", "strong": "The laughter rolled across the room like a wave over sand, and I studied the scratches on my desk until the bell rang.", "why": "The weak version names the feelings. The strong version shows them through sound and a small physical action, so the reader feels the embarrassment instead of being told about it."}}, {"prompt": "<p>In no more than <b>300 words</b>, write a <b>narrative</b> in which a young person learns something important from an older relative without a word being spoken.</p>", "title": "The Line", "essay": ["{{1|Grandpa Cyril spoke perhaps ten words a day, and none of them were meant for me.}}", "I was fifteen, sulking over a failed Mathematics test, when Mummy sent me to spend the August holidays with him in Gouyave. Every morning before dawn, we pushed his blue pirogue, the <i>Faith Again</i>, into the sea. {{2|The jetty smelled of diesel and nutmeg, and old Mr Bain always shouted the same joke. \"Cyril, you carrying the boy again? He go frighten all the fish!\"}}", "Grandpa only nodded.", "{{3|On the fourth morning, I tangled the handline into a bird's nest. I cursed, flung it onto the floor of the boat and folded my arms.}} {{4|\"This is pointless,\" I muttered. \"I'm not good at anything.\"}}", "{{5|Grandpa did not answer. He picked up the knot, sat on the cooler and began to work at it with thick, scarred fingers. One loop. Then another. The sun climbed. Waves slapped the hull.}} At last, he handed the line back, half undone, and gazed at the horizon.", "I finished it myself. It took twenty minutes.", "{{6|An hour later, the line jerked and burned my palm. A kingfish, silver and furious, was fighting below us. I hauled until the nylon sang. Then I felt his hand rest lightly on my wrist.}}", "{{7|Loosen. Wait. Breathe.}}", "I let the fish run, drew it back, let it run again. When it finally slid into the boat, Grandpa's eyes creased at the corners, and he gave one small nod.", "{{8|That nod meant more than any certificate. He had taught me that patience is not doing nothing; it is the steady, unhurried work of untangling what frustration has knotted.}}", "{{9|Grandpa Cyril said nothing to me that morning. It was the most he had ever told me.}}"], "notes": ["Orientation and hook: the grandfather's silence is established in one sentence, setting up the wordless lesson the prompt requires.", "Sensory detail (diesel and nutmeg) roots the setting in Grenada, while Grenadian Creole in dialogue adds local colour and humour.", "Complication shown through action: the narrator's frustration is revealed by what he does, not by naming the emotion.", "Brief dialogue reveals the narrator's low self-belief, which is the real problem the story resolves.", "Short sentences and fragments slow the pacing, so the reader experiences the grandfather's patience in real time.", "Climax: vivid verbs and sound (\"the nylon sang\") raise the tension, and a single touch replaces spoken instruction.", "Three one-word sentences translate the non-verbal cue into meaning, showing how the narrator \"hears\" the touch.", "Reflection links the knot and the fish to a mature insight about patience without preaching.", "The ending echoes the opening, turning the grandfather's silence from distance into the story's most powerful message."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Content", "of": 10, "got": 9, "why": "An original story that meets the wordless-lesson requirement through touch, gesture and example, with a clear insight about patience and non-verbal communication. One mark held back: the failed test is mentioned but never revisited; one line linking the lesson back to it would complete the story's purpose."}, {"part": "Organization", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "Two parallel tests (the knot and the fish) build logically to a climax and resolution; pacing is skilfully varied, and the final line circles back to the first."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 8, "why": "Precise, sensory Standard English narration, purposeful fragments for effect, and Creole used only in dialogue for authenticity."}], "upgrade": {"weak": "My grandfather showed me how to be patient and I understood what he meant even though he did not say anything.", "strong": "Then I felt his hand rest lightly on my wrist. Loosen. Wait. Breathe.", "why": "The weak version summarises the lesson. The strong version lets the reader feel the gesture and decode its meaning alongside the narrator, which is exactly what non-verbal communication requires."}}, {"prompt": "<p>In no more than <b>300 words</b>, write a <b>narrative</b> about a performance that goes wrong and what the narrator learns from it.</p>", "title": "Eyes Open", "essay": ["{{1|I had practised the arrangement so often that I could play it with my eyes closed. That was the problem.}}", "For three months, our steel orchestra from Laventille had rehearsed every night in a panyard lit by a single bulb. {{2|I played lead tenor, and the captain, Uncle Roddy, kept warning me. \"Doh rush it, Shani. Leh the music breathe.\"}}", "I nodded, but I did not really listen.", "{{3|On Panorama night, the Savannah stage glowed under white lights. The crowd smelled of corn soup and sweat, and the rhythm section shook the boards beneath my feet.}} We began well. Then, at the bridge, I shut my eyes, felt the rush of the crowd and played faster.", "{{4|Too fast. The other tenors stumbled. The basses dragged behind. The music split in two, like a road after an earthquake.}}", "{{5|My stick slipped from my sweaty fingers and clattered across the stage.}}", "For one terrible second, I froze. Then I forced my eyes open. {{6|Uncle Roddy was facing me, his palm held flat and low, his head nodding slowly: one, two, three, four.}} I grabbed my spare stick, watched his hand and came back in on the downbeat. Around me, the band drew together again, note by note.", "We placed sixth. As we loaded the racks onto the truck, the bass player squeezed my shoulder. {{7|\"Yuh come back, girl. Dat is what matter.\"}}", "{{8|I learned that night that a band is not a solo performed beside other people; it is a conversation, and conversation depends on listening and watching as much as on playing. I had been talking so loudly that I could not hear anyone else.}}", "{{9|I still know every note of that arrangement by heart. Now, though, I always play with my eyes open.}}"], "notes": ["Orientation and hook: an apparent strength is revealed as a weakness, creating immediate suspense.", "Trinidadian Creole in dialogue characterises the captain and plants the warning that the climax will prove right.", "Sensory detail (sight, smell and touch) recreates the atmosphere of Panorama at the Savannah.", "Short, clipped sentences quicken the pacing at the moment the performance falls apart, and the simile shows the scale of the failure.", "Climax: a single concrete image captures the narrator's worst moment without any explanation.", "Non-verbal communication resolves the crisis: the captain's gesture and nod restore the tempo, linking the story to the theme.", "Brief dialogue offers comfort and shows the band's values of recovery and solidarity.", "Reflection uses an extended metaphor of conversation to state the insight about listening without preaching.", "The ending echoes the opening, turning \"eyes closed\" into \"eyes open\" to show how the narrator has changed."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Content", "of": 10, "got": 10, "why": "Fully answers the prompt with a vivid performance failure, a believable recovery and a thoughtful insight about listening and non-verbal cues in a group."}, {"part": "Organization", "of": 7, "got": 6, "why": "Clear arc with a strong hook and circular ending. One mark held back: the resolution moves quickly from the recovery to the result; one sentence showing the band's reaction on stage would smooth the transition to the reflection."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 7, "why": "Controlled Standard English narration, effective sentence variety and Creole limited to dialogue. One mark held back: \"like a road after an earthquake\" is slightly forced; an image drawn from the panyard or the music itself would be sharper."}], "upgrade": {"weak": "I started playing too fast and everyone got confused and the song sounded really bad.", "strong": "Too fast. The other tenors stumbled. The basses dragged behind. The music split in two.", "why": "The weak version reports the problem in one flat sentence. The strong version uses short, rhythmic sentences that speed up the pacing, so the reader feels the performance collapse as it happens."}}]}, "p3": {"reflective": [{"year": "2026", "topic": "Natural disasters", "audience": "Homeowners", "form": "Personal essay", "prompt": "<p>3. (a) Write a reflective OR expository piece of between 200 and 250 words based on the topic <b>NATURAL DISASTERS</b>. Your piece should be suitable for an audience of <b>homeowners</b>.</p>", "title": "The Roof My Father Nailed Down", "essay": ["{{1|The morning after Hurricane Irma, I stood in what had been my living room in Cole Bay and looked straight up at the sky.}} The galvanise and rafters my father had nailed down decades earlier were somewhere across the lagoon, and I felt, absurdly, that I had let him down.", "For years I had treated the house as something finished. {{2|Every June, I told myself the shutters could wait one more season, and every November I felt lucky rather than prepared.}} Irma ended that habit in a single night.", "{{3|Rebuilding taught me what the storm could not.}} My contractor, Mr. Arrindell, showed me where the old roof had failed: {{4|not in the sheets themselves, but at the joints, where nothing held the rafters to the walls.}} Hurricane straps, he explained, cost little compared with a new roof. {{5|I felt foolish, then angry and, finally, strangely relieved, because a weakness I could see was a weakness I could fix.}}", "Today my roof is strapped and my shutters fit. {{6|Yet the greatest change is not in the house; it is in me.}} I no longer ask whether a storm will come, but whether I will be ready.", "{{7|So, to every homeowner who has ever said \"next season\", I ask the question I wish someone had asked me: if the wind came tonight, which part of your house would fail first?}} {{8|My father built this home to shelter us. Rebuilding it, I finally learned how to protect it.}}"], "notes": ["A precise, first-person sensory moment (looking up at open sky) places the reader inside a specific remembered experience at once.", "Balanced parallel clauses (\"Every June... every November\") expose the writer's old habit of complacency, which the essay will examine.", "A short, paradoxical topic sentence signals the turn from the disaster to the reflection on what it taught.", "Concrete, practical detail gives homeowners useful knowledge while keeping it inside the personal story rather than a lecture.", "A sequence of feelings (foolish, angry, relieved) shows thoughts and emotions being examined, the heart of reflective writing.", "The shift in understanding is stated explicitly: the real change is in the writer's attitude, not only the building.", "Pathos and direct address: the rhetorical question makes each homeowner picture their own roof, engaging the audience directly.", "The circular ending returns to the father from the opening and compresses the essay's insight into a memorable antithesis (shelter / protect)."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Creativity", "of": 10, "got": 9, "why": "An original personal angle (the father's roof) and a direct, engaging appeal to homeowners. One mark held back: rebuilding after a hurricane is a familiar scenario; next step: develop one less expected detail, such as an object salvaged from the old roof, to make the piece more idiosyncratic."}, {"part": "Organisation", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "A vivid introduction, a clear development from complacency to lesson to change, and a circular conclusion; each paragraph moves the reflection forward."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 8, "why": "A controlled, sincere first-person register suited to a personal essay; accurate grammar and punctuation, including the colon and semicolon, and varied sentence lengths."}], "justification": {"text": ["{{1|The purpose of this personal essay is to move homeowners, through reflection, to prepare for hurricanes before disaster strikes; the shift from feeling \"lucky rather than prepared\" to a strapped roof models the change I want readers to make.}} {{2|The intended audience is Sint Maarten homeowners, addressed directly in the closing rhetorical question, which uses pathos to make them picture their own roofs.}} {{3|The essay would appear in the June hurricane-preparedness supplement of a local daily newspaper, in print and online, when homeowners plan repairs for the season.}}"], "notes": ["Purpose: states what the piece aims to do and proves it with a quoted feature from 3(a).", "Intended audience: names a specific group and links a deliberate rhetorical appeal (pathos) to a feature of the essay.", "Context of communication: gives where (Sint Maarten), when (June, start of the season) and the channel (newspaper supplement, print and online)."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Purpose", "of": 5, "got": 5, "why": "A clear reflective-persuasive purpose supported by a quotation from the essay that shows the intended change in the reader."}, {"part": "Intended audience", "of": 5, "got": 5, "why": "A precise audience, with the rhetorical question and its appeal to pathos named as the feature that engages them."}, {"part": "Context of communication", "of": 5, "got": 5, "why": "Where, when and through which channel are all stated, and the timing is justified by the homeowners' needs."}]}, "upgrade": {"weak": "Hurricane Irma was very bad and it destroyed my house. Homeowners should always be prepared for hurricanes because they are dangerous.", "strong": "Every June, I told myself the shutters could wait one more season, and every November I felt lucky rather than prepared. Irma ended that habit in a single night.", "why": "The weak version reports the event and gives generic advice. The strong version examines the writer's own past behaviour honestly, which is what makes a piece reflective rather than merely informative."}}, {"year": "2026", "topic": "Natural disasters", "audience": "Homeowners", "form": "Memoir extract", "prompt": "<p>3. (a) Write a reflective OR expository piece of between 200 and 250 words based on the topic <b>NATURAL DISASTERS</b>. Your piece should be suitable for an audience of <b>homeowners</b>.</p>", "title": "The Bank Behind the House", "essay": ["{{1|For eleven years I ignored the bank of red earth behind my kitchen in the hills of Dominica.}} It was simply there, and when my neighbour, Mr. Laville, warned me, {{2|\"Dat bank go come down one day, you hear?\"}}, I laughed and offered him cocoa tea.", "Then came Tropical Storm Erika and rain without pause. By midnight I could hear the hillside groaning like an old floorboard. {{3|At two o'clock, a wet slap shook the back wall and mud pushed under the door like something alive.}}", "The bank had slipped. It stopped a metre from the house, held back by the roots of the mango tree I had planned to cut down. {{4|I sat on the front step until dawn, shaking, not from cold but from realising how close I had come and how little I had done.}}", "{{5|What I understood that morning has stayed with me: a house on a hillside does not end at its walls.}} The drain I never cleared, the slope I had cut back and the trees I had dismissed as clutter: {{6|all of it was part of my home, and all of it had been quietly asking for attention.}}", "Now I clear the drains every month, vetiver grass lines the slope and the mango tree stays. {{7|Fellow homeowners, listen to the land behind you before the rain forces you to listen.}} {{8|Mr. Laville still drinks cocoa tea on my step; now I am the one asking his advice.}}"], "notes": ["The opening confession establishes the memoir's reflective stance: the writer looks back on a past mistake from a wiser present.", "Dominican English is kept inside dialogue only, giving the neighbour an authentic voice while the narration stays in Standard English.", "Personification and onomatopoeic diction (\"slap\", mud \"like something alive\") recreate the remembered moment vividly.", "The writer examines the feeling precisely, rejecting the obvious cause (cold) for the true one (realisation), which deepens the reflection.", "The shift in understanding is placed at the start of the paragraph as a clear, quotable insight.", "A cumulative list resolved by repetition (\"all of it... all of it\") turns ordinary neglect into the piece's central lesson.", "Direct address and a chiasmus-like reversal (\"listen... forces you to listen\") engage homeowners and give the advice a memorable shape.", "The ending reverses the opening roles of the two neighbours, showing the narrator's growth through action rather than explanation."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Creativity", "of": 10, "got": 10, "why": "A distinctive memoir voice, the ironic detail of the mango tree that saves the house, and a role reversal at the end make the piece original and engaging for homeowners."}, {"part": "Organisation", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "Chronological development from warning to disaster to insight to changed behaviour, with a conclusion that circles back to the opening relationship."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 7, "why": "Vivid, controlled Standard English with effective figurative language. One mark held back: the long list sentence in paragraph four delays its main clause; next step: shorten the list or begin with the main idea so the sentence reads more cleanly."}], "justification": {"text": ["{{1|The purpose of this memoir extract is to make homeowners reflect on warning signs on their own land, shown through the narrator's shift from laughing at Mr. Laville to seeking his advice.}} {{2|The audience is homeowners on Dominica's hillsides; the direct address \"Fellow homeowners\" and the fear in the landslide scene appeal to pathos.}} {{3|It would be read aloud on a national radio programme at the start of the rainy season, reaching rural households while there is still time to clear drains.}}"], "notes": ["Purpose: links the aim of the memoir to the narrator's change, a specific feature of 3(a).", "Intended audience: identifies the group precisely and names pathos as the deliberate appeal, citing two features.", "Context of communication: states the channel (radio), where (Dominica, rural households) and when (start of the rainy season)."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Purpose", "of": 5, "got": 5, "why": "A precise reflective purpose supported by a specific feature of the memoir."}, {"part": "Intended audience", "of": 5, "got": 5, "why": "A clearly defined audience, with a named rhetorical appeal and quoted evidence of how it engages them."}, {"part": "Context of communication", "of": 5, "got": 4, "why": "Where, when and channel are stated. One mark held back: the answer does not explain why radio suits a written memoir; next step: add that a spoken reading reaches homeowners who may not read newspapers, and that Mr. Laville's dialogue comes alive aloud."}]}, "upgrade": {"weak": "I learned that landslides are dangerous and that people should take care of their land.", "strong": "What I understood that morning has stayed with me: a house on a hillside does not end at its walls.", "why": "The weak version states a general lesson anyone could write. The strong version ties the insight to the remembered moment (\"that morning\") and expresses it as a fresh image, which shows a genuine shift in understanding."}}, {"year": "2027", "topic": "Non-communicable diseases in children", "audience": "Parents", "form": "Reflective blog post", "prompt": "<p>3. (a) Write a reflective OR expository piece of between 200 and 250 words based on the topic <b>NON-COMMUNICABLE DISEASES IN CHILDREN</b>. Your piece should be suitable for an audience of <b>parents</b>.</p>", "title": "What My Fridge Door Was Telling Me", "essay": ["{{1|Last Saturday I counted the empty bottles in our recycling bin in St. Philip, and the number made me sit down.}} Almost every one had held a sweet drink, and almost every one had been my ten-year-old son's.", "I want to be honest with you, fellow parents, because I was not a careless mother. {{2|I packed the lunch kit, I checked the homework and I bought the \"juice\" because the label showed fruit.}} I thought a sweet drink was a treat. {{3|I never noticed that, in our house, the treat had quietly become the habit.}}", "Jaden was sluggish after school, and at his last check-up the doctor gently used the word \"overweight\". {{4|I nodded politely in her office and then, on the drive home, bought him a sweet drink to cheer him up.}} Writing that sentence still stings.", "{{5|What shifted for me was not fear but recognition: my son was not choosing these drinks; I was choosing them for him.}} Children copy what they see, and what Jaden saw in our fridge was colour and sugar.", "So we changed small things. Water now sits on the top shelf, mauby is a Sunday drink made with less sugar, and {{6|Jaden guards a jug of cucumber water that he insists is \"the best drink in Barbados\".}}", "{{7|Tonight, open your fridge and ask yourself what your child sees first.}} {{8|Mine used to see sugar. Now Jaden sees a choice I am finally proud of.}}"], "notes": ["A concrete, everyday image (counting bottles) opens the blog with a specific remembered moment rather than a general warning.", "A triad of responsible actions builds the writer's credibility (ethos) before she admits her mistake.", "The contrast between \"treat\" and \"habit\" names the hidden problem in plain, memorable language.", "An uncomfortable confession, followed by a short reflective sentence, shows the writer examining her own behaviour honestly.", "The shift in understanding is expressed through balanced clauses that move responsibility from the child to the parent.", "A light, humorous detail in the child's own words keeps the blog warm and shows the change working.", "An imperative call to action suits the blog form and engages parents directly.", "The ending echoes the fridge image and contrasts \"used to\" with \"now\" to close the reflection on a hopeful note."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Creativity", "of": 10, "got": 10, "why": "A fresh, conversational blog voice, a confessional honesty that parents can trust and a unifying fridge image make the piece original and highly engaging."}, {"part": "Organisation", "of": 7, "got": 6, "why": "A strong opening and a circular conclusion. One mark held back: \"So we changed small things\" jumps quickly from insight to action; next step: add a linking sentence about how the family decided what to change."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 8, "why": "An informal but correct register suited to a parenting blog, with accurate punctuation (colon, semicolon, quotation marks) and effective variety of sentence length."}], "justification": {"text": ["{{1|The purpose of this reflective blog post is to help parents recognise unhealthy drink habits early; my confession about buying a sweet drink after the check-up invites readers to examine their own choices.}} {{2|The audience is Barbadian parents of primary-school children, addressed as \"fellow parents\"; my honesty builds ethos, while the closing instruction about the fridge appeals to pathos.}} {{3|It would be posted on a local parenting blog and shared in school parents' WhatsApp groups in September, when lunch kits are packed for the new term.}}"], "notes": ["Purpose: states the aim and supports it with a specific feature (the confession) from 3(a).", "Intended audience: defines the readers precisely and names two deliberate rhetorical appeals, ethos and pathos.", "Context of communication: gives the channels (blog, WhatsApp groups), where (Barbados schools) and a well-chosen time (September)."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Purpose", "of": 5, "got": 5, "why": "A clear purpose, proved with a precise reference to the confession in the blog post."}, {"part": "Intended audience", "of": 5, "got": 5, "why": "A specific audience, with two rhetorical appeals linked to quoted features of the piece."}, {"part": "Context of communication", "of": 5, "got": 5, "why": "Channel, place and timing are all stated, and the September timing is justified by what parents are doing then."}]}, "upgrade": {"weak": "Parents should stop giving their children sugary drinks because they are unhealthy and can cause diseases.", "strong": "What shifted for me was not fear but recognition: my son was not choosing these drinks; I was choosing them for him.", "why": "The weak version lectures the reader. The strong version reflects on the writer's own role, so parents arrive at the lesson themselves, which is more persuasive and is what the reflective genre requires."}}, {"year": "2027", "topic": "Non-communicable diseases in children", "audience": "Parents", "form": "Journal entry", "prompt": "<p>3. (a) Write a reflective OR expository piece of between 200 and 250 words based on the topic <b>NON-COMMUNICABLE DISEASES IN CHILDREN</b>. Your piece should be suitable for an audience of <b>parents</b>.</p>", "title": "Sunday Walks", "essay": ["{{1|Sunday, 14 March. Six weeks of walking, and tonight, for the first time, Aaliyah asked if we could go again tomorrow.}} I am writing it down so I never forget.", "When Dr. Grant told us that Aaliyah's check-up showed a high blood sugar warning, I heard only the word \"warning\" and saw only my fault. {{2|Twelve years old. Twelve years of patties after school, fried chicken on Fridays and car rides to places she could have walked to.}} I drove home in silence.", "{{3|That first walk around Mandeville was miserable.}} Aaliyah sulked, my knees complained and her brother asked why we were \"being punished\". {{4|If I am honest with this journal, he was right: I had turned her health into a sentence, not a choice.}}", "Things changed slowly. We let the children choose the route. {{5|We counted mango trees, argued about cricket and, one evening, laughed so hard at a goat blocking the road that we had to stop altogether.}} {{6|Somewhere along those roads, the walks stopped being about her blood sugar and started being about us.}}", "{{7|I see now that a child's habits grow in the soil a parent prepares, and I am learning to prepare it better.}} The doctor will check her again next month. I am anxious, but not afraid.", "{{8|Note to self, and to any parent who reads this one day: do not wait for a warning to start walking together.}}"], "notes": ["The dated opening and present-moment detail establish the journal form and begin with a hopeful turning point.", "Fragments and repetition (\"Twelve years... Twelve years\") capture the parent's guilt as it was felt, a hallmark of private reflection.", "A blunt, short topic sentence admits failure honestly, which builds trust with the parent reader.", "The writer examines the child's comment and draws a precise insight, using the antithesis \"a sentence, not a choice\".", "A list of specific, humorous moments shows rather than tells how the family's experience changed.", "The shift in understanding is captured in balanced phrasing (\"about her blood sugar... about us\").", "An extended metaphor (soil a parent prepares) universalises the lesson without lecturing.", "Direct address breaks out of the private journal to speak to parents, a deliberate and memorable closing appeal to pathos."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Creativity", "of": 10, "got": 10, "why": "The journal form is used idiosyncratically, moving from private guilt to a final note addressed to other parents; humour and honest detail make it original and moving."}, {"part": "Organisation", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "Opens at the turning point, flashes back to the diagnosis, develops chronologically through the walks and concludes with insight and a closing note, all coherently linked."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 8, "why": "An intimate yet correct register suited to a journal, with deliberate fragments for effect, accurate punctuation and skilful figurative language."}], "justification": {"text": ["{{1|The purpose of this journal entry is to reassure parents whose child has received a health warning that small, shared changes help, which is why it moves from guilt to hope.}} {{2|The intended audience is Jamaican parents; the private, dated form builds trust, and the closing \"Note to self, and to any parent\" appeals to pathos by inviting them into the family's experience.}} {{3|It would appear in the Sunday health section of a national newspaper, in print and online, on a weekend when families plan their routines.}}"], "notes": ["Purpose: states the aim and refers to the structural shift from guilt to hope in 3(a).", "Intended audience: identifies the readers and explains how the journal form and a quoted closing line create a deliberate appeal to pathos.", "Context of communication: gives the channel (newspaper health section, print and online), where (Jamaica) and when (Sunday)."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Purpose", "of": 5, "got": 5, "why": "A clear, specific purpose linked to the piece's movement from guilt to hope."}, {"part": "Intended audience", "of": 5, "got": 4, "why": "A rhetorical appeal is named and supported with a quotation. One mark held back: \"Jamaican parents\" is broad; next step: narrow it to parents of children recently given a health warning, as the purpose already implies."}, {"part": "Context of communication", "of": 5, "got": 5, "why": "Channel, place and timing are all stated, and the Sunday timing is linked to how families plan their week."}]}, "upgrade": {"weak": "Today we went for a walk again. It was nice and Aaliyah liked it. Walking is good exercise for children.", "strong": "Somewhere along those roads, the walks stopped being about her blood sugar and started being about us.", "why": "The weak version records events and states a fact. The strong version reflects on what the events meant, showing the change in the parent's understanding that a reflective journal entry needs."}}], "expository": [{"year": "2026", "topic": "Natural disasters", "audience": "Homeowners", "form": "Explanatory guide with subheadings", "prompt": "<p>3. (a) Write a reflective OR expository piece of between 200 and 250 words based on the topic <b>NATURAL DISASTERS</b>. Your piece should be suitable for an audience of <b>homeowners</b>.</p>", "title": "Before the Wind Comes: A Homeowner's Guide to Hurricane Readiness", "essay": ["{{1|Few homeowners in the British Virgin Islands have forgotten the sound of Hurricane Irma tearing at roofs in 2017.}} The Atlantic hurricane season runs from 1 June to 30 November, and the quiet weeks before it are the best time to prepare. {{2|This guide explains four tasks: protecting openings, securing the roof, safeguarding documents and recording your belongings for insurance.}}", "<b>1. Protect windows and doors</b>", "{{3|Wind that enters through a single broken window can pressurise a house and help lift the roof from the inside.}} Fit storm shutters, or cut marine plywood to size now. Label each panel and store its screws with it, so fitting takes minutes, not hours.", "<b>2. Strap down the roof</b>", "Hurricane straps are metal connectors that tie the roof frame to the walls. Ask a qualified builder to check that every rafter is strapped. {{4|The cost of a few straps is small compared with the cost of replacing an entire roof.}}", "<b>3. Waterproof your documents</b>", "Seal deeds, insurance policies and passports in plastic bags inside a waterproof box. {{5|Then email scanned copies to yourself or to a relative overseas.}}", "<b>4. Photograph before you need to</b>", "{{6|Walk through each room with your phone, recording appliances, furniture and the outside of the house.}} Clear records help insurers settle claims faster. Check that your policy covers both wind and flood damage.", "{{7|Shutters, straps, documents and photographs: four tasks, one weekend.}} {{8|Complete them before June, and when the next warning is broadcast, you can listen calmly, knowing your home is ready.}}"], "notes": ["A shared local memory of Hurricane Irma hooks Virgin Islands homeowners immediately and establishes why the guide matters.", "The overview previews the four tasks in the exact order of the numbered subheadings, a clear expository roadmap.", "Cause-and-effect explanation tells the reader why shutters matter before telling them what to do, which is the heart of exposition.", "A deliberate appeal to logos: weighing a small cost against a large one speaks directly to a homeowner's budget.", "A practical, specific instruction addressed as 'you', suited to a guide that readers will act on.", "Concrete, step-by-step detail turns a general idea (insurance) into an action any homeowner can complete.", "A memorable four-part summary in a short, punchy fragment recaps every section without adding new information.", "The closing appeal to pathos replaces the fear of the opening with calm reassurance, giving the guide a satisfying circular shape."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Creativity", "of": 10, "got": 9, "why": "The guide form is used purposefully: numbered, action-led subheadings, a local Irma hook and a reassuring close engage homeowners well. One mark held back: the subheadings are conventional; a more distinctive device, such as a tick-box checklist line under each section, would make the form more idiosyncratic."}, {"part": "Organisation", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "A clear introduction that previews four tasks, four labelled sections in the promised order and a conclusion that recaps them; each section explains the reason before the action."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 8, "why": "Register suits a practical guide: direct imperatives, plain technical terms explained in context (hurricane straps) and accurate, carefully proofread Standard English throughout."}], "justification": {"text": ["{{1|The purpose of this guide is to explain how homeowners can prepare their houses before the hurricane season, so numbered subheadings and short imperatives make each task easy to follow and complete.}} {{2|The audience is homeowners in the British Virgin Islands, many of whom lived through Irma; the opening memory and the logical appeal about the cost of a new roof address their concerns about safety and money.}} {{3|The guide would appear in May, in the community council's printed preparedness booklet and on its social media pages, before the season opens on 1 June.}}"], "notes": ["Purpose is stated precisely (to explain and prepare) and linked to specific features of the piece: subheadings and imperatives.", "Audience is identified specifically and justified with two named features, including the deliberate logos appeal.", "Context covers where (booklet and social media), when (May) and through which channel (print and online), all three required elements."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Purpose", "of": 5, "got": 5, "why": "Clear expository purpose, explicitly connected to the guide's numbered subheadings and imperative style."}, {"part": "Intended audience", "of": 5, "got": 5, "why": "A precise audience (homeowners in the British Virgin Islands) justified by two specific features of the piece and their concerns."}, {"part": "Context of communication", "of": 5, "got": 5, "why": "Where, when and channel are all given, and the May timing is justified by the start of the season."}]}, "upgrade": {"weak": "You should get shutters because hurricanes are very dangerous and can damage your house badly.", "strong": "Wind that enters through a single broken window can pressurise a house and help lift the roof from the inside, so fit shutters before June.", "why": "The weak version simply warns. The strong version explains the cause and effect behind the advice, which is what makes a piece expository and earns Creativity and Organisation marks."}}, {"year": "2026", "topic": "Natural disasters", "audience": "Homeowners", "form": "Feature article", "prompt": "<p>3. (a) Write a reflective OR expository piece of between 200 and 250 words based on the topic <b>NATURAL DISASTERS</b>. Your piece should be suitable for an audience of <b>homeowners</b>.</p>", "title": "The Ground Beneath Our Homes: What Caribbean Homeowners Should Know About Earthquakes and Tsunamis", "essay": ["{{1|Hurricanes arrive with days of warning; earthquakes arrive with none.}} The Caribbean plate grinds against the North and South American plates, making the region seismically active. {{2|History offers sober reminders: Kingston, Jamaica, in 1907 and Haiti in 2010.}} Understanding how these hazards affect a house is the first step towards reducing their impact.", "{{3|During an earthquake, much of the danger comes not from the shaking itself, but from what falls.}} Block walls without enough steel reinforcement can collapse, while unsecured water heaters and gas cylinders can topple. {{4|Homeowners can reduce this risk by asking a structural engineer to check reinforcement, bolting tall furniture to walls and storing heavy items on low shelves.}}", "Tsunamis, usually triggered by undersea earthquakes, are rarer but threaten coastal homes from Gros Islet in Saint Lucia to Scarborough in Tobago. {{5|Natural warning signs are worth memorising: a strong or long-lasting earthquake, a sudden fall in sea level that exposes the seabed, or an unusual roar from the ocean.}} If any occurs, do not wait for an official alert; move at once to high ground.", "{{6|Preparation, therefore, is a family project.}} Each household should agree on an evacuation route and meeting point, and practise 'Drop, Cover and Hold On'. Keep a grab bag with water, a torch, medication and copies of documents beside the door.", "{{7|We cannot predict when the ground will move.}} {{8|We can, however, decide how well our homes, and the people inside them, will stand when it does.}}"], "notes": ["A balanced antithesis contrasts a familiar hazard with an unfamiliar one, hooking readers who think only about hurricanes.", "Two well-established historical events give the article credibility (ethos) without inventing statistics.", "A topic sentence that corrects a common misconception, an effective way to open an explanatory paragraph.", "Three concrete, affordable actions turn explanation into practical knowledge for homeowners.", "A triad of warning signs presented as a list is easy for readers to remember in an emergency.", "A short linking sentence with 'therefore' shows how the explanations lead logically to preparation.", "A blunt, short sentence admits the limits of science, which makes the article honest and authoritative.", "The inclusive 'we' and the image of people inside homes create a deliberate appeal to pathos in a memorable close."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Creativity", "of": 10, "got": 10, "why": "A genuine feature-article voice: an arresting antithesis, real Caribbean history and places, and an emotive yet restrained close engage homeowners while staying informative."}, {"part": "Organisation", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "Moves logically from why the region is at risk, to earthquake damage, to tsunamis, to preparation and a conclusion; transitions such as 'therefore' and 'however' maintain coherence."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 7, "why": "Accurate, formal Standard English with precise vocabulary. One mark held back: the compressed history sentence ('Kingston, Jamaica, in 1907 and Haiti in 2010') omits what happened; a brief verb such as 'earthquakes devastated' would make it clearer."}], "justification": {"text": ["{{1|This feature article aims to inform homeowners about how earthquakes and tsunamis threaten Caribbean houses and what they can do to reduce the risk.}} {{2|Its audience is adult homeowners across the region, so references to Kingston, Haiti and Saint Lucia, and practical steps such as bolting furniture, suit readers responsible for a property.}} {{3|It would be published in the home and safety section of a regional newspaper's weekend edition and online in early November, around World Tsunami Awareness Day on 5 November, as the hurricane season ends and attention can shift to other hazards.}}"], "notes": ["Purpose combines two aims (inform about the threat and explain responses) that match the article's structure.", "Audience is justified with named Caribbean references and a specific practical feature.", "Context names the section, publication, channel (print and online) and a reasoned time of publication."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Purpose", "of": 5, "got": 5, "why": "Purpose is precise and mirrors the two halves of the article."}, {"part": "Intended audience", "of": 5, "got": 4, "why": "Audience is linked to specific references and actions. One mark held back: 'adult homeowners across the region' is broad; naming coastal homeowners would match the tsunami section more precisely."}, {"part": "Context of communication", "of": 5, "got": 5, "why": "Publication, section, channel and a reasoned timing tied to World Tsunami Awareness Day are all given."}]}, "upgrade": {"weak": "Earthquakes are very scary and can happen anytime in the Caribbean so everybody must be careful.", "strong": "The Caribbean lies along the edges of the Caribbean tectonic plate, and this movement makes the region seismically active.", "why": "The weak version relies on fear and vague words like 'careful'. The strong version explains the cause clearly and objectively, which is the defining feature of expository writing."}}, {"year": "2027", "topic": "Non-communicable diseases in children", "audience": "Parents", "form": "Newsletter column", "prompt": "<p>3. (a) Write a reflective OR expository piece of between 200 and 250 words based on the topic <b>NON-COMMUNICABLE DISEASES IN CHILDREN</b>. Your piece should be suitable for an audience of <b>parents</b>.</p>", "title": "Parents' Corner: What's Really in the Lunch Kit?", "essay": ["{{1|A single bottle of sweet drink can contain more sugar than a child should have in an entire day.}} This month, Parents' Corner explains how sugary drinks and processed snacks contribute to childhood obesity and type 2 diabetes, and what families can do.", "<b>Why drinks matter</b>", "{{2|Sugar in drinks is absorbed quickly, yet it does not make children feel full.}} As a result, they take in extra calories without eating less, and those calories are stored as fat. {{3|Type 2 diabetes, once known as 'adult-onset' diabetes, is now being diagnosed in children,}} and excess weight is one of its main risk factors.", "<b>The snack trap</b>", "Cheese puffs, sweet biscuits and flavoured crackers are often high in salt, sugar and fat but low in fibre. {{4|Because they are cheap, convenient and heavily advertised, they can quietly replace the fruit and ground provisions that earlier generations ate.}}", "<b>Small swaps that work</b>", "{{5|The encouraging news is that small, steady changes make a real difference.}} {{6|Fill the lunch kit with plain water or coconut water instead of box drinks, and add a local fruit such as a banana, a mango or a portugal.}} Read labels: ingredients are listed from largest amount to smallest, so if sugar is near the top, choose something else. {{7|Most importantly, eat the same way yourself, because children copy what they see at the family table.}}", "{{8|The lunch kit you pack tomorrow morning is a small decision; repeated every school day, it becomes a lifelong habit.}}"], "notes": ["A striking but well-established fact hooks parents instantly without relying on a statistic.", "The topic sentence explains a cause clearly, then the next sentence traces its effect with 'As a result'.", "A general, well-established medical fact gives the column credibility (ethos) and raises the stakes for parents.", "A cause-and-effect sentence explains why processed snacks spread, linking health to Caribbean food culture.", "A positive turn reassures readers, keeping the column constructive rather than alarming.", "Specific Caribbean swaps (coconut water, portugal) make the advice practical and locally grounded.", "A direct appeal to parents' role as models, a deliberate appeal to their sense of responsibility.", "The closing antithesis ('small decision' against 'lifelong habit') uses pathos to motivate action and is easy to remember."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Creativity", "of": 10, "got": 9, "why": "A warm, conversational column voice, a named regular feature and local food references engage parents well. One mark held back: a short 'Did you know?' aside or a reader question would exploit the newsletter form more distinctively."}, {"part": "Organisation", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "An introduction that previews the content, three labelled sections moving from problem to solution, and a one-sentence conclusion; cause-and-effect links keep it coherent."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 8, "why": "A friendly but correct register for a school newsletter; sentences are clear, varied and accurately punctuated, including the semicolon in the final line."}], "justification": {"text": ["{{1|This column aims to explain to parents how sugary drinks and processed snacks increase their children's risk of obesity and type 2 diabetes, and to suggest simple swaps.}} {{2|The audience is parents of primary school pupils in Trinidad, so the lunch-kit focus, local fruits such as portugal and the appeal to parents as role models address their daily responsibilities.}} {{3|It would appear in the PTA's monthly newsletter, sent home in book bags and shared on the school's WhatsApp group in September, when families are planning school lunches for the new term.}}"], "notes": ["Purpose is stated precisely and matches both halves of the column: explanation and advice.", "Audience is narrowed to a specific group and justified with three named features, including the rhetorical appeal.", "Context gives where, two channels and a well-reasoned time of distribution."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Purpose", "of": 5, "got": 5, "why": "A clear, two-part expository purpose tied directly to the column's content."}, {"part": "Intended audience", "of": 5, "got": 5, "why": "Specific audience justified with concrete features of the piece."}, {"part": "Context of communication", "of": 5, "got": 5, "why": "Where, when and channel are all stated and the September timing is justified."}]}, "upgrade": {"weak": "Parents need to stop giving their children sweet drinks because it is very bad and they will get sick.", "strong": "Sugar in drinks is absorbed quickly, yet it does not make children feel full, so they take in extra calories without eating less at meals.", "why": "The weak version scolds and makes a vague claim. The strong version explains how the problem happens, which informs parents and respects them, the tone a Grade One expository piece needs."}}, {"year": "2027", "topic": "Non-communicable diseases in children", "audience": "Parents", "form": "Explanatory radio talk script", "prompt": "<p>3. (a) Write a reflective OR expository piece of between 200 and 250 words based on the topic <b>NON-COMMUNICABLE DISEASES IN CHILDREN</b>. Your piece should be suitable for an audience of <b>parents</b>.</p>", "title": "Family Matters: Switching Off to Switch On", "essay": ["[SOUND: A phone notification chime. Fade under.]", "PRESENTER: Good morning, parents, and welcome to Family Matters. {{1|Hear that chime? In many homes from Bridgetown to Belmopan, it is the most familiar sound of the evening.}} {{2|Today: how screen time and inactivity affect children's health, and three routines that help.}}", "{{3|First, the body.}} Children who sit for hours with a tablet burn far less energy than children who swim or play cricket in the yard. {{4|Health experts recommend at least sixty minutes of physical activity every day.}} Inactivity is linked to weight gain, which raises the risk of type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure later in life.", "Second, sleep. {{5|The bright light from screens can delay the body's natural signal to sleep,}} so a child scrolling at ten o'clock may struggle to concentrate in class next morning.", "So, what can families do? {{6|Routine one: create screen-free zones, beginning with the dinner table and the bedroom. Routine two: swap an hour of screen time for movement: a beach walk, a living-room dance or road tennis. Routine three: switch devices off an hour before bed.}} {{7|And parents, join in: children follow routines the whole family shares.}}", "{{8|Screens are here to stay. With simple routines, though, they can stay in their place while our children move, rest and grow.}} This has been Family Matters. Until next week: switch off, and get moving.", "[SOUND: Jingle.]"], "notes": ["A sound cue and a rhetorical question exploit the radio medium, making listeners recognise their own homes.", "The overview previews the two problems and the solutions, so listeners know what to follow.", "A short spoken signpost suits radio, where listeners cannot reread or see headings.", "A widely accepted health guideline supports the explanation (ethos) without inventing figures.", "A clear cause-and-effect link connects screens to a consequence parents notice: tiredness at school.", "Numbered spoken routines with Caribbean activities such as road tennis act as audible subheadings.", "Direct address to parents is a deliberate appeal to their influence and responsibility.", "The triad 'move, rest and grow' gives the talk a warm, memorable close that appeals to parents' hopes for their children."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Creativity", "of": 10, "got": 10, "why": "An idiosyncratic use of the radio genre: sound cues, a named segment, a rhetorical question, spoken signposts and a catchy sign-off keep listeners engaged throughout."}, {"part": "Organisation", "of": 7, "got": 7, "why": "Opening, two explained problems signposted 'First' and 'Second', three numbered solutions and a conclusion; the structure is easy to follow by ear."}, {"part": "Expression", "of": 8, "got": 7, "why": "Conversational yet accurate register suited to radio. One mark held back: the colon-heavy 'Routine two' line is awkward to read aloud; a simple list sentence would flow better on air."}], "justification": {"text": ["{{1|This talk aims to explain how screen time and inactivity affect children's health and to give parents three practical routines.}} {{2|The audience is Caribbean parents, so the presenter addresses them directly and appeals to their role in shaping family habits.}} {{3|It would be broadcast on a national radio station's morning family health segment at about 7:15 a.m., when parents are driving their children to school.}} {{4|Spoken signposts and sound cues suit this purely audio channel.}}"], "notes": ["Purpose is precise and reflects both the explanation and the advice in the script.", "Audience is justified with a specific feature: direct address and the appeal to parents' influence.", "Context gives the channel, programme and exact time, with a reason that fits the audience's routine.", "A final link between the channel and specific script features shows awareness of medium."], "breakdown": [{"part": "Purpose", "of": 5, "got": 5, "why": "Clear expository purpose linked to the content of the talk."}, {"part": "Intended audience", "of": 5, "got": 4, "why": "Audience is identified and supported by one feature. One mark held back: 'Caribbean parents' is broad; naming the parents of primary school children and citing the road tennis or bedtime examples would be sharper."}, {"part": "Context of communication", "of": 5, "got": 5, "why": "Where, when and channel are all specified, and the choice of channel is justified by named features of the script."}]}, "upgrade": {"weak": "PRESENTER: Screen time is bad for kids, so you need to limit it. That is all for today.", "strong": "PRESENTER: Routine one: create screen-free zones, beginning with the dinner table and the bedroom.", "why": "The weak version gives a vague instruction with no explanation or structure. The strong version uses a spoken signpost and a concrete, achievable action, which suits both the radio form and an expository purpose."}}]}};
